(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"su-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Long Way Back","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"The road back from the music festival took twice as long because Greg refused to admit the van was lost.\n\n“I’m not lost,” he said for the fourth time.\n\n“You just asked a cow for directions.”\n\n“The cow was near a sign.”\n\n“The cow was eating grass.”\n\nThe cow wasn't the only one inconvenienced by our navigation. At the next crossroads Greg pulled into a small service station, where a woman was sorting cold drinks into a refrigerator that clearly needed repairing.\n\n“Hey,” he said, leaning in through the doorway. “Any chance the coastal highway's this way?”\n\nShe looked out at the van, at his hand-drawn directions and at the embarrassed grin he wore whenever he knew he'd made something worse.\n\n“The road you're on goes back to the quarry,” she said. “Where did you get that map?”\n\nGreg unfolded it. The paper crackled.\n\nShe laughed. “Oh, honey. That bridge was washed out six years ago.”\n\n“I've been driving on optimism and expired cartography.”\n\n“Mostly expired cartography.” She drew a route on the back of a receipt, then added a warning about the gravel turn after the old mill.\n\n“Want me to buy something so this isn't a free consultation?” he asked.\n\n“You can buy something because you're thirsty,” she said. “Directions are still free.”\n\nI liked her immediately. We bought two bottles of water and a packet of crackers because we actually needed them. Greg stood at the counter wondering whether to tip for the map. She told him the jar was for the local fire company and that the donation was voluntary. He put in a little cash without making a show of it.\n\nBack in the van, he fixed the receipt to the dashboard with a bit of tape.\n\n“You know, I could've figured that out,” he said.\n\n“You asked a cow first.”\n\n“A very promising cow.”\n\nI snorted and reached across the bench seat. He slid his hand into mine without looking away from the road.\n\n“I like this better,” he admitted.\n\n“Being lost?”\n\n“Being able to ask when I am.”\n\nHe squeezed my fingers. Then he missed the turn by about thirty feet, pulled over safely, and looked at me with such miserable comic timing that I started laughing before he could speak.\n\n“It had a thoughtful expression.”\n\nI held the paper map on my lap and tried not to laugh. The festival had ended beneath a pink evening sky. Lila had played a brilliant set. Greg had introduced me to old friends, new musicians, and one man who had made a three-hour argument about harmonicas sound like a spiritual experience.\n\nI had danced with Greg in the grass. I had eaten fried dough. I had listened to him play a new song and felt my whole body recognize the moment before the first verse ended.\n\nNow the van's engine made a worrying noise.\n\n“It sounds like a drawer full of rocks,” I said.\n\n“That’s normal.”\n\n“It is not.”\n\n“It’s a vintage sound.”\n\n“The van is trying to die.”\n\n“It has a flair for drama.”\n\nThe engine coughed. Greg guided the van onto the shoulder. A sign announced that the nearest town was twelve miles away.\n\nHe turned off the ignition.\n\nSilence filled the cabin.\n\n“I’m going to say something,” I began.\n\n“Please don’t say the van is dead.”\n\n“The van is dead.”\n\nGreg rested his forehead against the steering wheel.\n\n“You said it first,” I added.\n\n“I know.”\n\nThe sun lowered toward the fields. Cars passed occasionally, their headlights flashing across the windows. Greg opened the hood and stared into the engine compartment with the confidence of a man who had learned just enough about machines to become dangerous.\n\n“Can you fix it?” I asked.\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“That word is not mechanically reassuring.”\n\nHe found a loose belt, a cracked hose, and a tool he swore had not been in the van when they left. Nothing made the engine willing to restart.\n\n“We’ll call for help,” he said.\n\nHe tried his phone. No signal.\n\nI tried mine. One bar appeared, then vanished.\n\nThe familiar pressure began in my chest.\n\nGreg saw it. “Hey.”\n\n“I’m fine.”\n\n“I didn’t ask.”\n\nI leaned against the van. “We’re stranded.”\n\n“Temporarily.”\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“No. But I know we have water, food, blankets, and two phones that will eventually find civilization.”\n\n“What if they don’t?”\n\n“Then we become legends of the cow road.”\n\nI tried to laugh. It came out wrong.\n\nGreg wiped his hands on a rag and came to stand in front of me. “Do you want a plan?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“We wait until the next car passes, then flag it down if it’s safe. If it doesn’t, we walk toward the town while staying on the shoulder. We keep the lights on until the battery says otherwise. We eat the sandwiches before they become science.”\n\n“You have a plan.”\n\n“I have several.”\n\n“Why didn’t you say that?”\n\n“You were looking at the engine like you wanted it to apologize.”\n\n“It should.”\n\n“I agree.”\n\nThe next car stopped. The driver called a tow service and offered them a ride to the town. Greg stayed close to me the entire time, his hand resting lightly against my back whenever the road became uneven.\n\nAt the small motel, the clerk gave me a room with two beds and a window facing a field. The tow truck would come in the morning.\n\n“We can take the separate beds,” Greg said.\n\nI looked at him. “Do you want to?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then don’t.”\n\nHe smiled, but the nervousness did not leave his eyes.\n\nThe room was clean, plain, and too quiet after the festival. I showered first. When I returned, Greg sat on one bed with a paper cup of motel coffee.\n\n“Your turn,” I said.\n\n“I’m afraid the shower will judge me.”\n\n“The cow already did that.”\n\nHe disappeared into the bathroom. The water turned on. I opened the curtains, then closed them when a moth threw itself against the glass.\n\nWhen Greg came out, his hair was damp and his shirt clung to his shoulders. I looked away too late.\n\n“You’re staring,” he said.\n\n“You’re wet.”\n\n“That sounds like an observation.”\n\n“It is.”\n\nHe sat beside me. “Are you okay?”\n\n“I’m tired.”\n\n“That’s true.”\n\n“And embarrassed.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because the van broke down and I immediately panicked.”\n\n“The van broke down. Panicking was a reasonable response.”\n\n“You made a plan.”\n\n“I panicked too. I just did it under the hood.”\n\nI turned toward him. “You did?”\n\n“I have no idea what half those parts do.”\n\n“You looked confident.”\n\n“I had a rag.”\n\nThe laughter came easier this time. Greg leaned back against the headboard.\n\n“I used to think I had to be the person who knew what to do,” he said. “Especially when I was younger. If I was funny enough, useful enough, calm enough, maybe nobody would notice I was terrified.”\n\n“I notice.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Does that bother you?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because you don’t use it against me.”\n\nThe words made me look at him.\n\nGreg reached for my hand. “There’s a difference between being seen and being exposed.”\n\nI turned my palm upward. He laced his fingers through mine.\n\n“I want to be seen by you,” he said. “Even when I’m not impressive.”\n\n“You asked a cow for directions.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nI kissed him. The motel room softened around the edges. Greg's hand rose to my cheek, then moved to the back of my neck. He held me close, warm and familiar.\n\nThe bed creaked when I shifted. Greg pulled back just enough to look at me.\n\n“Still okay?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you want—”\n\n“I want you to stop asking questions for thirty seconds.”\n\nHe smiled. “I can try.”\n\n“Try now.”\n\nHe kissed me again.\n\nThe motel room filled with the quiet sounds of affection. We talked until we were too tired to keep our eyes open and fell asleep with the television muttering softly in the background.\n\nIn the morning, the tow truck arrived. The van was repaired with a part the mechanic described as “mostly compatible.” Greg paid, thanked him, and promised not to ask livestock for directions again.\n\nOn the road home, my hand rested in his.\n\n“The trip was good,” I said.\n\n“Even the breakdown?”\n\n“Especially the breakdown.”\n\nGreg glanced at me. “You’re developing an alarming appreciation for chaos.”\n\n“I’m developing an appreciation for what you do when chaos arrives.”\n\nHis thumb brushed over my knuckles.\n\nThe van carried me toward Beach City, toward the bakery and the boardwalk and the couch with a blanket waiting on it.\n\nIt had broken down on the way there.\n\nIt was still carrying me home.\n\nThe scenic route passed through a town with a clock tower, three antique shops, and a bakery that advertised pies in the window. Greg pulled over before I could ask.\n\n“This is not our route,” I said.\n\n“The route has become more interesting.”\n\n“The route is a road.”\n\n“Roads have personalities.”\n\n“You and the van are both impossible.”\n\nHe bought a pie. The baker asked whether I was newlyweds. Greg choked on a laugh. I answered that I was simply trying to survive a road trip.\n\n“That was a missed opportunity,” Greg said once I was outside.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“A very specific joke.”\n\n“You can tell it to the cow.”\n\n“The cow would understand.”\n\nI ate pie on a bench beneath the clock tower. The town moved around me slowly: a dog pulling at its leash, an old couple arguing about directions, a teenager carrying a stack of books.\n\n“Do you ever think about living somewhere like this?” I asked.\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“A small town. A little house. A kitchen that doesn’t move.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“Would you?”\n\nGreg looked at the pie in his hands. “I could. I’m not sure I would be happy if I did it because someone else thought it would make me easier to love.”\n\nI nodded. “I understand that.”\n\n“I could also keep the van and visit.”\n\n“You’d park it in the driveway.”\n\n“It would be a guest house.”\n\n“It has delusions again.”\n\n“It has aspirations.”\n\nThe conversation did not resolve where either of us would live. It made room for the possibility that home could take different shapes over time.\n\nWhen I reached Beach City that evening, Greg parked outside my apartment instead of his.\n\n“Do you want to come up?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You sound tired.”\n\n“I’m tired and I want you.”\n\nThe directness made me smile.\n\nInside, I played music quietly and shared the last of the pie. Greg washed the plates while I unpacked the blankets.\n\n“The van survived,” he said.\n\n“Barely.”\n\n“A lot like us.”\n\nI crossed the room and kissed him. “We’re doing better than barely.”\n\nHe held me close. “We are.”\n\nThe night unfolded with fewer jokes and more honesty. We talked until sleep became unavoidable, then held each other in the quiet afterward.\n\nIn the morning, Greg left a slice of pie beside my coffee and called it a road-trip blessing.\n\nWe ate it together.\n\nSlowly.","word_count":1842},"su-17":{"number":17,"title":"The Almost Goodbye","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"The almost goodbye arrived in an email with a subject line that said: WE WOULD LIKE TO OFFER YOU THE POSITION.\n\nI read it three times before telling Greg.\n\nThe job was in another city. It paid more than the community center. It came with a stable schedule, health benefits, and a small apartment included for the first six months. It was the sort of opportunity people were supposed to be grateful for.\n\nI sat at the kitchen table with my laptop open. Greg stood at the counter, slicing an apple into uneven pieces.\n\n“That’s good,” he said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It sounds good.”\n\n“It does.”\n\nHe finished cutting the apple and set the knife down.\n\n“When do they need an answer?”\n\n“Friday.”\n\nGreg missed the compost container with an apple core. He stared at it on the floor, then at me.\n\n\"Well, that's a metaphor I'm refusing to take personally.\"\n\nI laughed in spite of myself. He picked it up and dropped it where it belonged before sitting at the table.\n\n\"Does the offer say who you'd be working with? Real people, I mean, not the corporate version where everyone is a passionate family.\"\n\nI scrolled through the attachment. There was a salary, but no staffing plan. There was an apartment, but no explanation of whether I could keep the lease if the position ended.\n\n\"Huh,\" he said. \"I played enough gigs for exposure to know a nice-sounding offer can leave out the expensive parts.\"\n\n\"You are not negotiating my career for me.\"\n\n\"Buddy, I can barely negotiate with the van. I just want you to get the whole song before you agree to play.\"\n\nHe slid the pencil across the table. I made a list of questions about hours, sick leave, staff support, and housing. The email took four minutes to write. The choice didn't become easy, but at least it was my choice and not a guess.\n\n\"If I move,\" I said, \"I don't stop being ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I still need room to be honest with the people I love, including you.\"\n\nGreg nodded. \"Yeah. We've talked about that. Distance doesn't turn honesty into a special occasion.\"\n\nHe glanced at the screen again. \"Ask about parking, too.\"\n\n\"For you?\"\n\n\"For a hypothetical retired rock star whose van is a work of art.\"\n\nI took his hand under the table and kissed his knuckles. He grinned, a little shy beneath the joke.\n\nThe deadline remained Friday. We had a few more questions, and I had somebody beside me who wasn't trying to answer them in my place.\n\n“That’s soon.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nGreg sat across from me. He looked at the email, then at my face.\n\n“Do you want it?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“That’s okay.”\n\n“It would be good for me.”\n\n“Good is not the same as wanted.”\n\n“You always say things like that when I’m trying to be practical.”\n\n“Practical is important. It just shouldn’t be the only voice in the room.”\n\nI closed the laptop. “What if I take it?”\n\nGreg's fingers curled around the edge of the table.\n\n“Then we figure out what that means.”\n\n“You would do long distance?”\n\n“I would try.”\n\n“You might hate it.”\n\n“I might.”\n\n“You might meet someone else.”\n\n“I might.”\n\nI looked away.\n\nGreg reached across the table but stopped before touching me. “I’m not going to tell you not to take the job.”\n\n“Would you want me to stay?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe word hurt.\n\n“Then why aren’t you asking me to?”\n\n“Because I want you to stay because you want this life, not because I made leaving painful.”\n\n“You make everything sound simple.”\n\n“It isn’t simple. It’s just clear.”\n\nThe email waited behind the closed laptop.\n\nFor three days, I tried to decide. I went to work. I answered questions. I played music with Greg. I slept beside him and woke before he did, watching dawn move across his face.\n\nEverywhere, the choice followed.\n\nAt the community center, Mara found me staring at a box of drumsticks.\n\n“You’re leaving,” she said.\n\nI blinked. “I haven’t decided.”\n\n“That wasn’t what I asked.”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“You’re using Greg's word now.”\n\n“He doesn’t own it.”\n\n“Neither do you.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nMara leaned against the supply cabinet. “Do you want the job?”\n\n“It would be stable.”\n\n“That is not what I asked.”\n\n“It would be good for my future.”\n\n“That is also not what I asked.”\n\nI turned the drumstick in my hand. “I don’t know how to want something without proving it makes sense.”\n\nMara's expression softened. “Then start with whether you want to wake up there.”\n\nThe question stayed with me all the way home.\n\nThat evening, I found Greg in the van, replacing one of the moon-shaped hooks. He looked up when I climbed inside.\n\n“The hook is not broken,” I said.\n\n“It was emotionally loose.”\n\nI sat on the bed. “I got the email.”\n\n“The answer is due tomorrow.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nGreg put down the screwdriver.\n\n“I think I want to take it,” I said.\n\nHe inhaled slowly. “Okay.”\n\n“You’re not going to say anything?”\n\n“What do you want me to say?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“There’s that word again.”\n\nI looked toward the open doors. “I want you to ask me to stay.”\n\nGreg's face changed.\n\n“I want you to tell me I’m making a mistake,” I continued. “I want you to make it impossible for me to leave so I can blame you if I do.”\n\nHe moved closer. “I won’t.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I love you.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I want you here.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But I don’t want to become another person who decides that my fear is more important than your freedom.”\n\nThe tears came before I could stop them.\n\nGreg crouched in front of me. “Can I touch you?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe took my hands. “If you go, I will miss you. I will be scared. I will probably write an extremely embarrassing song about highway exits.”\n\n“That sounds terrible.”\n\n“It will be.”\n\n“You might not wait.”\n\n“I can’t promise I’ll stay exactly the same. I can promise I won’t pretend I’m fine if I’m not.”\n\n“And if I stay?”\n\n“Then I’ll still ask what you want next month. You don’t have to turn staying into a lifetime sentence.”\n\nI looked at the space I had made in the van: my books, my scarf, the metal pick.\n\n“I’m afraid that if I stay, I’m choosing you instead of myself.”\n\n“Then don’t choose me,” Greg said. “Choose the life that lets you be yourself. If I’m in it, I’ll be grateful. If I’m not, I’ll survive.”\n\nThe kindness felt like a blade.\n\nI leaned forward and pressed my forehead to his.\n\n“I don’t want to go,” I whispered.\n\nGreg closed his eyes.\n\n“I want the job because it’s good,” I continued. “But I want to wake up here. I want the bakery noise. I want the community center. I want your terrible songs.”\n\n“They’re not all terrible.”\n\n“Most.”\n\n“That’s fair.”\n\n“I want you.”\n\nHis hands tightened around mine. “Are you sure?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You can still change your mind.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe kissed me. The van was quiet except for the ocean and the soft creak of the mattress.\n\nThe kiss became warmer. Greg asked. I answered. He moved slowly, giving me every chance to stop, and I held him closer instead.\n\nThe doors closed. The email waited, unanswered, while the two of us chose the present with open eyes.\n\nIn the morning, I declined the job.\n\nNot because Greg had asked me to stay.\n\nBecause I had finally asked myself where I wanted to wake up.\n\nThe answer was Beach City.\n\nFor now.\n\nThe next morning, I went to the community center and told Mara I had declined the position.\n\nShe listened while arranging a box of percussion instruments.\n\n“Do you regret it?” she asked.\n\n“Not today.”\n\n“That is the answer you have.”\n\n“I’m trying to stop demanding answers from the future.”\n\nMara handed me a tambourine. “A sensible policy.”\n\nGreg arrived at noon with pastries and a paper map.\n\n“I have a proposal,” he announced.\n\n“Is it about a road trip?”\n\n“It is about several road trips, one festival, and an emergency pastry reserve.”\n\n“You are responding to my career decision with baked goods.”\n\n“I am responding with joy.”\n\nHe spread the map across the table. There were stars drawn beside towns, beaches, and music halls.\n\n“You don’t have to plan my life,” I said.\n\n“I’m planning experiences. You can reject all of them.”\n\n“Why are you doing this?”\n\n“Because I want a future with you that has more than one shape.”\n\nThe words made me quiet.\n\nGreg tapped one of the stars. “You can work here. You can travel. You can change your mind. We can be together without making your whole life fit inside my van.”\n\n“That is a very good plan.”\n\n“I have been practicing.”\n\nI folded the map. “I choose the lake.”\n\nHis smile appeared immediately. “The lake it is.”\n\n“And the festival.”\n\n“Both?”\n\n“I contain multitudes.”\n\nHe kissed me in the empty hallway.\n\nThe job offer did not vanish. It remained in my email, a reminder that opportunity could exist without being accepted. Beach City was not the only possible future.\n\nIt was the one I wanted now.\n\nThat distinction made staying feel like freedom instead of fear.\n\nThat night, I wrote my own list. Reasons to stay. Reasons to travel. Things I wanted independent of Greg. Things I wanted with him.\n\nThe list was messy. Some reasons contradicted each other. I wanted roots and roads. Stability and surprise. A room of my own and a place beside his.\n\nGreg read it only when I offered.\n\n“This is a good life,” he said.\n\n“It is a complicated one.”\n\n“Those are not opposites.”\n\nI folded the paper. “I am not choosing you instead of myself.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I am choosing a self that includes you.”\n\nHis eyes softened. “That is the best kind of choice.”\n\nI put the list on the refrigerator. Greg added a magnet shaped like a pancake.\n\n“This is not a serious household system,” I said.\n\n“It is a flexible household system.”\n\n“You are hiding wisdom behind breakfast.”\n\n“Breakfast has carried me through many difficult decisions.”\n\nI kissed him in the kitchen. The future remained complicated, but it was no longer a single road with Greg standing at the end of it.\n\nIt was a map I could both hold.\n\nI placed it beneath the pancake magnet. Greg stood behind me with his arms around my waist.\n\n“I like this,” he said.\n\n“The map?”\n\n“The way you stay without disappearing.”\n\nI turned and kissed him.\n\nThe pancake magnet held the map in place.\n\nIt stayed there through breakfast, through the afternoon, and through another quiet conversation about what I wanted next.\n\nWe both listened.\n\nThe map stayed.","word_count":1809},"su-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Work of Staying","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"The application had been sitting under the car wash receipts for two days.\n\nGreg wasn't snooping. He was looking for the electricity bill, which was somehow more embarrassing. He lifted the envelope, saw my name, and put it back on the table without opening it.\n\n\"That looks important,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Want to tell me?\"\n\nI looked at the plastic chair across from him. I wanted to say it wasn't decided, which was true, and leave the rest until I knew what I wanted. But he would notice me being careful with every word.\n\n\"I got offered a job,\" I said. \"Not here.\"\n\nHe smiled immediately. \"That's fantastic.\"\n\n\"It's across the water. I'd have to spend longer stretches away from Beach City.\"\n\n\"Still fantastic.\"\n\nThe fluorescent light buzzed above us. I watched him straighten a stack of receipts that didn't need straightening.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You can be happy for me and still have feelings about the distance.\"\n\nHe stopped moving. \"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why are you pretending this is as easy as finding a new parking spot?\"\n\nHe looked down. \"Because I was afraid if I said I'd miss you, it'd sound like I was asking you to give it up.\"\n\nThere it was. The thing beneath the joke. I pulled out the other chair.\n\n\"Sit?\"\n\nHe sat. The electricity bill fell from his hand and landed upside down between us.\n\n\"I'm not asking permission,\" I said. \"I am asking whether you want to talk about what this would mean for us.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I do.\"\n\nWe started with the stupidly practical parts because they were the parts we could actually do something about. The job paid enough to cover travel if I didn't spend it all getting back and forth for one-night visits. Greg had bookings on certain weekends, including a school fundraiser he'd already promised to play. I had other partners and commitments beyond Beach City; none of those disappeared because a job offer existed.\n\n\"I know you've been clear about being ethically non-monogamous,\" he said. \"And that you prefer polyamory. I guess I keep thinking a schedule's gonna make me sound like I'm booking a slot in your life.\"\n\n\"You are in my life. A schedule just means I don't promise to be in two places on the same Thursday.\"\n\nHe laughed softly. \"That'd make a good song.\"\n\n\"Only if you leave the receipts out of it.\"\n\nWe wrote down what we could promise. We would tell each other about changes early enough to matter. Neither of us would treat silence as a punishment. We could say we wanted more time without using guilt to get it. Visits would be invitations, not surprise tests. If either of us couldn't make something work, we would talk about it instead of announcing a failure.\n\nWhen we finished the first page of our plan, I went to the board by the sink and wrote down the dates I'd have to answer the job offer. I needed an actual deadline, not one I could move whenever the decision made me nervous.\n\nGreg looked at it. \"So there's an interview?\"\n\n\"Yes. A second one. I didn't tell you because I didn't know whether I wanted to go.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"I want to find out what the work is like when nobody's trying to impress me.\"\n\nHe thought about that, then nodded.\n\nWe made a list of questions I could ask without surrendering the chance to accept. Would the job pay for travel when I needed to cross the water? Who approved a schedule change? Would I be expected to take on duties that belonged to people already working there? If I chose to leave after the trial period, would I still be able to come back without being treated as a traitor?\n\nGreg laughed at the last one. \"That sounds like a lot of your life.\"\n\n\"Also sounds like I should've checked the bus timetable before offering to drive you every weekend,\" Greg said.\n\nHe pulled a car-wash receipt across the desk and did sums on the back. Fuel, the van's increasingly unreliable starter, his own shifts and the days Steven might need him. No grand promises. Just ugly arithmetic.\n\nHe tried adding the numbers again, as though a different pencil mark might persuade the van to run on good intentions.\n\n“Okay,” he said. “Problem. If I drive out Friday night and come back Sunday, I miss the Saturday morning wash shift. I can ask Mara to swap, but she has a life, so I should probably ask before I draw an entire love story on a receipt.”\n\n“That's growth.”\n\n“Don't tell Steven. He'll think I'm becoming responsible.”\n\nI was laughing when his phone buzzed. It was a message from Steven asking whether Greg could help move a box of things out of the old storage room over the weekend.\n\nGreg showed it to me, then frowned at his penciled timetable.\n\n“See? There it is. Something I actually said I'd do.”\n\n“You can tell him you're busy.”\n\n“I can, but I already told him yes. And I want to be there.” He looked embarrassed. “He's grown up, but that doesn't mean I'm done being his dad.”\n\n“I don't want you to be.”\n\nGreg made a face at the numbers, tore off the first version and began again. In the new plan he had space to help Steven, work his shift and visit me another day if my interview went well. The travel estimate wasn't especially romantic. It also didn't require anyone to break a promise.\n\nWhen he finished, he turned the receipt over to hide the calculations. On the blank side he drew a tiny van with a heart on its roof.\n\n“Patent pending,” he said. “The Universe Mobile. Runs on affection and questionable mechanics.”\n\n“That last part I believe.”\n\nHe gave me a look of theatrical betrayal. I leaned across the desk to kiss him, and the paper slid off the table between us. We both reached for it at once and knocked our heads together.\n\n“See?” he groaned. “Terrible engineering.”\n\nI kissed his forehead this time. “Excellent research data.”\n\n\"Two trips, maybe three,\" he said. \"More if the van figures out how to become a boat.\"\n\n\"I didn't ask you to be my ferry.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I know.\" He tapped four fingers on the paper, paused, and tried a different rhythm. \"I do this thing where I volunteer before I check whether I can show up.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I had a whole speech planned. Super supportive boyfriend, excellent hair, totally cool about distance.\"\n\n\"Your hair wasn't part of the interview.\"\n\n\"Well, it should be.\"\n\nHis grin faded.\n\n\"I don't want you to turn it down,\" he said. \"I also don't want to wave goodbye and spend a month hearing every missed call as a sad song.\"\n\n\"Then don't write the song alone.\"\n\n\"Oh, I started it. It's terrible.\" He pushed the receipt toward me. \"This is better. Boring numbers, real phone calls, me actually showing up.\"\n\nI wrote the interview date beside his fuel estimate, leaving room to change both. Greg returned the electricity bill to the top of his stack; that was what he'd been looking for in the first place.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe stood, opened the little office window, and let in air that smelled of soap and summer asphalt. The car wash machinery had stopped for the day. The silence made the room feel like someplace we could choose to be.\n\n\"Can I tell you what scares me?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Not that you'll like the job. I hope you do. I think I'm scared I'll start treating your coming home like proof you still love me, and then every delayed bus will make me a jerk.\"\n\nI leaned against the desk. \"Then we have to notice when that starts happening. I can't guarantee I won't miss a visit. I can guarantee I won't make you guess whether I mean to come.\"\n\nHe looked toward the calendar.\n\n\"Could we plan the first week after the interview before either of us promises the whole year?\"\n\n\"That sounds perfect.\"\n\n\"Wow. You said perfect. Write that down.\"\n\nI did, in the margin of the page he was holding. He tried not to smile and failed.\n\nOur non-exclusive relationship wasn't a test of how much distance we could endure. It was two people trying not to let the fear of a future decision take the place of one we could make today.\n\nI asked whether he wanted to walk to the diner before we returned to the application. He did. He held out his hand, and when I took it he looked as pleased as he had the first time we played a song together.\n\nGreg pointed at the page. \"This feels very grown-up.\"\n\n\"Terrifying, isn't it?\"\n\n\"I was hoping love came with fewer columns.\"\n\nI nudged his knee with mine. \"You have a business. You know columns.\"\n\n\"That's different. The columns at the car wash don't have feelings.\"\n\n\"Your accounts would suggest otherwise.\"\n\nHe laughed, then went quiet.\n\n\"I do want you to stay,\" he said. \"I know I don't get to decide for you. But I want you to know that.\"\n\nThe admission made my throat tighten. There was something braver about hearing his preference without having to fight for my freedom first.\n\n\"Thank you,\" I said. \"I haven't decided.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"And if I take the job, I still want to come home here sometimes. Not because I owe you proof. Because I like this stupid office and your terrible coffee.\"\n\n\"Hey. The coffee is improving.\"\n\n\"It's learning to apologize.\"\n\nHe reached across the table. \"Can I hold your hand?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe sat with our fingers tangled between the bill and the job application. He didn't ask to see the letter. I didn't hand it over to prove I trusted him.\n\nAfter closing, we walked to the van. The air had turned cool. A light flickered in the car wash sign, and he made a note to call someone about it before the next wet morning.\n\nInside the van, he put on a quiet recording—not one of the old songs that felt too loaded, but a rough instrumental he'd made the previous week. I leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"Stay for dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Tonight.\"\n\n\"Tonight's pretty good.\"\n\nHe kissed me when I turned toward him. We had all the time we wanted for that moment, and no right to assume anything about the next one. Later, we went inside and let the night become ours.\n\nIn the morning, the application was still unanswered. I put it in the drawer beside my toothbrush. We would talk again when I knew more, not pretend a single night could settle a whole future.","word_count":1791},"su-42":{"number":42,"title":"Folding Chairs and Honest Labor","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"Greg had agreed to perform at a fundraiser before anyone told him there was no one assigned to put away the chairs.\n\nThat was how I found him at Starwake Pier, holding a box of tangled lantern cords in one hand and a folding table under his other arm.\n\n\"How's the glamorous life of a musician?\" I asked.\n\n\"Nobody warned me about furniture.\"\n\n\"Did you read the volunteer sheet?\"\n\n\"Yep.\"\n\n\"Did you put your own name next to every empty task?\"\n\nGreg glanced at the paper pinned to a post. Nearly every line had GREG written beside it.\n\n\"That is a wildly unfair accusation,\" he said. \"Some of those say 'Greg U.'\"\n\nHe was trying to make me laugh, which was ordinarily a successful strategy. I took the table from him instead.\n\n\"You're performing tonight. You also have sound checks, and you're supposed to make sure the speakers don't blow the fuse again. You can't do everything.\"\n\nGreg lowered the tangled lantern cords to the boards as if they might bite him.\n\n\"Okay. New plan. I play music, and someone qualified handles the part where nobody gets electrocuted.\"\n\nTamsin looked up from her inspection sheet. \"That's my part. And I need the proper cable, not an enthusiastic volunteer with a screwdriver.\"\n\n\"Music to my ears,\" Greg said. \"Well, technically that's music to my electrician.\"\n\nShe didn't laugh. She did point out the stage's unmarked access route, so we moved two speaker stands before anyone tripped over them.\n\nI crossed three names off Greg's volunteer assignments and asked Rowan about taking the first sound check. Rowan named a fee. Greg agreed to it without turning paid work into a touching story about community spirit.\n\n\"I'm in danger of becoming organized,\" he whispered.\n\n\"You still haven't found your capo.\"\n\nHe held up a clothespin. \"Technology is what you make of it.\"\n\nBy dusk, the equipment inventory had two signatures and the pay sheet listed actual hours. Greg brought a small tin of Iroh's citrus-and-smoke tea to the booth, the same batch he'd tasted at the Jasmine Dragon. I watched him read the brewing note twice and follow it anyway.\n\nOne sip made him wrinkle his nose. He took another.\n\n\"Your tea guy knows what he's doing,\" he said. \"Still tastes like a campfire got a music scholarship.\"\n\n\"Are you going to tell him that?\"\n\n\"Oh, absolutely. Then I'm gonna ask if he wants to hear my terrible guitar joke.\"\n\nI kissed him quickly, before the stage lights came up. He brushed his thumb over my hand and reached for his guitar, ready to be Greg and nobody else's version of wise.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't sound convinced.\"\n\nGreg set the cords down.\n\n\"People need the money. The pier needs new supports. If somebody says they'll do a job and then doesn't, I can at least make sure it happens.\"\n\n\"I know why you do it. That doesn't make the plan workable.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. The sun caught on the edges of his hair.\n\n\"Do you have a better plan?\"\n\n\"I was hoping we could make one together.\"\n\nWe began with the chairs. There were forty-eight of them, three with loose hinges and one whose left leg did not quite touch the ground. I wrote the number at the top of the volunteer sheet and set the broken ones aside.\n\nGreg inspected the crooked chair.\n\n\"Hey, this one's got character.\"\n\n\"This one has a lawsuit.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThe fundraiser was meant to pay for safer pier railings and a storage shed so equipment wouldn't sit exposed to saltwater all winter. Greg had offered music. Mara had offered food. Tamsin had offered to oversee the repairs. Somewhere between the promises, nobody had agreed who would collect donations or clean up afterward.\n\nI borrowed a pencil from Pip and crossed GREG off the tasks he hadn't been asked to own.\n\n\"Somebody still has to do those,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes. Somebody can volunteer.\"\n\n\"What if they don't?\"\n\n\"Then we scale down the event. We don't pretend you owe free labor to everyone because you know how to play a guitar.\"\n\nHe sat on the edge of the stage. His expression softened, then tightened.\n\n\"Sometimes it feels like if I say no, the whole thing falls apart.\"\n\n\"Then the thing was badly designed.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That's a weirdly comforting insult to an event.\"\n\n\"One of my specialties.\"\n\nWe spent an hour asking people what they could actually do. Katara said her clinic volunteers could help with refreshments for an hour, not all night. Danse agreed to check the temporary stage railings but would not sign off on the damaged western section. Iroh could supervise tea and cleanup until dusk; after that, he had an appointment of his own.\n\nNola arrived for the sound check carrying a case of cables and a schedule that looked significantly less forgiving than Greg's hopeful volunteer sheet.\n\n\"Which of these jobs is paid?\" she asked.\n\nGreg read the paper. \"Uh. None of them?\"\n\n\"Then my crew's only doing the work in the contract. We have another booking tomorrow morning.\"\n\nShe wasn't being cruel. She was watching two sound technicians haul expensive equipment across a damp pier while half the volunteers argued about a banner.\n\nGreg scratched his beard. \"I was going to do the teardown myself.\"\n\n\"You can't disconnect a stage you're still playing on,\" Nola said.\n\n\"Well, when you put it that way.\"\n\nMaui appeared carrying the marked stack of chairs. \"Fear not! The great Maui can clear the entire pier with one hand.\"\n\n\"Please use both,\" Tamsin called from the railing. \"And don't stack them in the exit.\"\n\nMaui looked as though somebody had insulted the artistry of his shoulders. Greg grinned and tried a little drumroll on a chair seat. The chair squealed.\n\n\"Don't give him percussion,\" I said.\n\nNola checked the booking sheet. She moved the sound test earlier and made a separate line for the load-out crew. Danse could check the approved stage railings, but the electrical load would still have to be signed off by the responsible technician.\n\nGreg listened to the new timing. \"So two sets, no mystery third set, and I don't become a chair company afterward.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Nola said.\n\nHe hummed a rising four-note phrase. It sounded like relief.\n\nMaui stopped beside us. \"Where's my name on the program?\"\n\n\"Under chairs,\" I told him.\n\nHe sighed. \"You people refuse to recognize an artist.\"\n\nMaui offered to carry every chair personally.\n\nI handed him the stack labeled FIFTEEN CHAIRS, TWO PEOPLE.\n\nHe read it.\n\n\"You're limiting my heroism.\"\n\n\"I'm improving its scheduling.\"\n\nThe sound check introduced a new problem.\n\nGreg's amplifier buzzed when the stage crew switched on the lantern string. Danse watched the voltage meter and asked to shut the shared circuit down until they could identify the load.\n\n“Hey, no argument here,” Greg said. “I've already had one amp go out with a pop. Not doing that on purpose.”\n\nMaui appeared beside them with a coil of cable balanced on his hook. “I can hang the lanterns from the tallest post. Boom. Problem solved.”\n\n“Problem relocated,” Danse corrected. “The post hasn't been inspected for that load.”\n\nMaui looked offended. “I've lifted islands.”\n\n“And we appreciate the resume,” Greg said. “But the island probably didn't have a building inspector.”\n\nI laughed; Maui did not. He put the cable down and asked how he could help without making the power problem worse. Nola directed him to the approved string of unlit lanterns at the back of the square. He carried them with the grave solemnity of someone demonstrating world-shaking restraint.\n\nGreg checked the amplifier's cable, turned the gain down, and waited while the qualified technician tested a separate circuit. The replacement power lead didn't reach as far as the original, so he agreed to a smaller stage area and changed the set list to suit the room.\n\n“You okay with being less loud?” I asked him.\n\n“Less loud?” he said, aghast. “Jasper, I can make an acoustic guitar sound embarrassing all by itself.”\n\nDanse recorded the permitted load. Maui came back carrying the folding chairs he'd been assigned. They both looked pleased with work that hadn't required a dramatic announcement.\n\nIt wasn't a contest over who could save the concert. People had come to hear music and raise money for repairs. We were trying not to give them another repair bill.\n\nAt noon we had six volunteers, a safe seating plan, and a stage that did not require anyone to stand on the broken section. Greg looked at the sheet, then at me.\n\n\"You know the terrible thing?\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"I do hate it when that happens.\"\n\nThe first guests arrived while Greg tuned his guitar. Some came from Beach City; others wandered over from the Lanternwell Square market. A child asked whether all the songs would be about doors. Greg promised at least one about chairs, which earned me a look from Mara that could have curdled milk.\n\nA man wearing a wide brown hat stopped beside the stage.\n\n\"You're Greg Universe, right?\"\n\n\"That's me.\"\n\n\"Great! Heard you were playing all night. Can you do another three songs after the fundraiser? My kid's a huge fan.\"\n\nGreg smiled out of reflex.\n\n\"Uh, maybe—\"\n\nI watched the word forming.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\n\"Actually,\" he said, \"I've agreed to two sets. I can sign the kid's program afterward if they want, but I've got to help with the equipment before heading home.\"\n\nThe man blinked.\n\n\"You're not doing an encore?\"\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\nFor a terrible second, I thought the man might argue. Instead, he shrugged and went to find seats.\n\nGreg turned toward me, looking almost surprised.\n\n\"That was uncomfortable.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And the sky did not fall.\"\n\n\"Good. We're running low on replacement sky.\"\n\nHe laughed, then reached for my hand. His fingers were rough against my palm from the guitar strings.\n\n\"You okay with me learning this slowly?\" he asked.\n\n\"I'd be suspicious if you learned it all at once.\"\n\nThe concert went well enough to feel like a real concert instead of a miracle. One microphone buzzed. The first song began a little late because a child dropped a cup in the front row. The audience sang the chorus of Greg's old road song louder than he did.\n\nHalfway through the second set, a light on the pier flickered.\n\nDanse stood at once.\n\nGreg stopped playing.\n\n\"Problem?\"\n\n\"Power fluctuation. Keep everyone off the western platform.\"\n\nThere was no panic. We had actually rehearsed what to do.\n\nMaui helped move chairs to the safer end. Katara brought the children to the square. Greg put down his guitar and checked the speaker plugs while Danse tested the line. They found a loose connection and decided not to restart the stage circuit.\n\nThe remaining songs would be acoustic.\n\n\"I can do acoustic,\" Greg announced to the audience. \"It's mostly what I do anyway.\"\n\nSomeone cheered.\n\nThe night finished with a small circle of people and no dramatic lighting. I preferred it. Greg's voice carried across the water, catching in the warm space between the lanterns. I could see how tired he was, and how happy he was that he hadn't spent the whole evening trying to be four people at once.\n\nWhen the final note faded, he looked at me over the strings.\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe smiled back.\n\nThen he stood up and helped fold the first chair.\n\n\"You're not allowed to do all of them,\" I reminded him.\n\n\"Just this one.\"\n\n\"Fine.\"\n\nWe stacked them in pairs, carried equipment to the dry storeroom, and counted the donation box with Mara present. The fundraiser would cover the railings, not the new shed. That was less than we'd hoped. It was enough to begin.\n\nThe last guest left with a paper cup of tea and the promise that the next event would have a proper cleanup roster.\n\nGreg and I walked toward the Starwake Door after midnight.\n\nHe was carrying only his guitar. I was carrying the repaired volunteer sheet, folded small enough to fit in my pocket.\n\n\"Good night?\" I asked.\n\n\"Best one I've had in a while.\"\n\n\"Even without an encore?\"\n\n\"Especially without one.\"\n\nHe tucked a loose strand of hair behind my ear. The touch was gentle, a question rather than a claim.\n\n\"Can I give you one private encore?\"\n\n\"Depends. Is it about chairs?\"\n\n\"No promises.\"\n\nI kissed him before he could invent a second verse.\n\nLater, when the van door closed behind us, we left the music and the rest of the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning, Greg found the volunteer sheet on the dashboard. Under my handwriting he'd added a new rule:\n\nGREG IS ALLOWED TO BE A MUSICIAN, NOT A FURNITURE COMPANY.\n\nI added one more line beneath it.\n\nJASPER IS NOT THE ENTIRE MANAGEMENT DEPARTMENT EITHER.\n\nHe kissed my cheek while I wrote.\n\nWe both liked the amended version.","word_count":2155},"su-48":{"number":48,"title":"An Instrument Returned","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"The guitar came back in a cardboard box.\n\nThat was never a good sign.\n\nGreg stood on the steps of Starwake Pier with the box in his hands and an expression that didn't quite know whether to laugh or cry. The instrument belonged to him. He'd lent it to a young musician named Rowan, who had promised to bring it back after the Listening Fair.\n\nRowan stood nearby, twisting their fingers together.\n\n\"I'm really sorry,\" they said.\n\nGreg opened the box.\n\nThe neck was intact, but the bridge had lifted from the body. One string was broken, and a long scratch ran along the lower edge where the finish had been scraped away.\n\nI felt my stomach drop.\n\n\"Oh, Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at the instrument for a long time.\n\nThen he put it down.\n\n\"What happened?\" he asked.\n\nRowan swallowed.\n\n\"The stand tipped when somebody moved a crate. I caught it before it hit the floor, but the case slipped. I thought I could fix the string, and then I saw the bridge.\"\n\n\"Did anyone get hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nGreg breathed out.\n\n\"Okay. That's something.\"\n\nRowan looked startled.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"I'm disappointed. It's an old guitar. I wanted it back in one piece.\"\n\n\"I can pay for the repair.\"\n\n\"Let's find out what it needs before we promise anything.\"\n\nI looked at him. He was upset, and he wasn't hiding it under a joke. That was new enough to make me proud.\n\nWe took the guitar to Brasswatch Open Workshop.\n\nDanse was running a training session on careful measuring. Renn had a row of small metal fittings laid out on the bench. When Greg arrived carrying the box, everyone paused.\n\n\"Musical instrument,\" Danse said.\n\n\"Broken musical instrument,\" Greg corrected.\n\nDanse examined the bridge.\n\n\"The adhesive has failed.\"\n\n\"Yes, but the wood might still be sound,\" Greg said.\n\nDanse reached for a tool.\n\nI touched his sleeve.\n\n\"Ask first.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"May I examine it?\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\nThe first ten minutes went badly.\n\nDanse wanted to measure every piece before deciding on a repair. Greg knew which parts of the guitar had always creaked and which changes would affect its sound. Both were right about something. Both kept interrupting the other.\n\n\"If you clamp it there, you'll change the resonance,\" Greg said.\n\n\"If I don't stabilize it, the bridge will lift again.\"\n\n\"I know, but—\"\n\n\"You haven't let me finish.\"\n\n\"Neither have you.\"\n\nI raised a hand.\n\n\"Would either of you like to hear the other person's sentence?\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\nRenn laughed.\n\n\"That seems fair.\"\n\nWe tried again.\n\nGreg explained how the instrument sounded before the damage and which imperfections were part of its history. Danse described the structural repair needed to make it safe to restring. Renn took notes and asked whether they could handle the small fittings under supervision.\n\nGreg took the guitar out of the box and showed Danse the shallow scratch near the lower edge. It was old, smooth to the touch, and nowhere near the broken bridge.\n\n“Leave that,” he said.\n\nDanse inspected it. “It doesn't affect the structure.”\n\n“Yeah. I know.”\n\n“Then it won't be included in the repair.”\n\nGreg looked at him for a moment, surprised that he hadn't needed to explain why a mark could matter without being damage. Then he pointed to the bridge.\n\n“This one is new. Listen when I pluck the low string. Hear that little rattle?”\n\nDanse leaned closer, listening with the concentration he usually gave a suspicious engine. “Yes. The bridge isn't seated.”\n\n“Right. And if you glue it down wrong, it'll hold but sound awful. Kind of like building a truck that drives but only in a straight line.”\n\nDanse considered the analogy. “A badly aligned truck would still be dangerous.”\n\n“Man, you really know how to make a metaphor file a safety report.”\n\nRenn laughed from the other bench. Danse gave them a look that would have silenced most Brotherhood recruits. It didn't work on Greg, who was already tapping a rhythm on the body of the guitar.\n\n“I can test the tone,” Greg offered. “You do the structural bit. We both know our end of this thing.”\n\nDanse set down the measuring tool. “Agreed. But you wait for me to certify the joint before you put tension on it.”\n\n“Deal.”\n\nThere was a little pause. Then Danse reached for Greg's hand, not to shake it but to look at the calluses across his fingers.\n\n“You play frequently?”\n\n“Every chance I get.”\n\nDanse turned the hand over and released it. “Then you should have an instrument that won't injure you.”\n\nGreg's grin softened. “Yeah. I'd like that.”\n\nBy afternoon they'd agreed on a plan.\n\nThe bridge could be reattached after the old glue was cleaned away. The damaged finish didn't need to be made invisible. The new string would be matched to the rest of the set. Greg would test the instrument before the repair was considered complete.\n\nRowan arrived after the inspection.\n\n\"How much?\" they asked.\n\nDanse showed them the estimate.\n\nRowan looked relieved.\n\n\"I can afford that if I pay in two parts.\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\n\"Then let's do that.\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Would you still lend me a guitar sometime?\"\n\nGreg looked at the box.\n\n\"Not this one for a while.\"\n\nRowan's face fell.\n\n\"But if you're still learning, I can help you find an instrument you can practice on. One that doesn't cost more than your rent.\"\n\nRowan smiled.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nI watched Greg carefully. It would have been easy for him to pretend the damage didn't matter because he wanted to be generous. Instead he'd asked for a repair and left room for a relationship that might become different.\n\nBefore the repair began, Rowan returned with a small envelope. They had counted out the first payment and wanted to know whether the rest could be paid after their next shift.\n\nGreg looked at Danse. Danse looked at the estimate.\n\n\"That is what the agreement says,\" Danse answered. \"We don't alter a signed repayment plan because someone seems anxious.\"\n\nRowan relaxed a little. I could see that they'd expected the adults in the room to be angry enough to demand everything at once.\n\n\"Can I help with the repair?\" they asked.\n\n\"Not on this instrument,\" Greg said gently. \"I want the workshop to do it. But you can watch from the marked area, if Danse says it's safe.\"\n\nDanse set out a clean tray, explained the hot tools and the safe path to the exit. He did not ask Rowan to handle expensive equipment in exchange for the privilege of being there. Renn took the scheduled shop shift; everyone whose time was being used would be paid according to their agreement.\n\nThe repair took longer than Greg wanted. He kept hovering in the doorway, then walking away, then returning with a new question he probably knew the answer to already.\n\nI found him by the water pump.\n\n\"You can be upset,\" I said.\n\n\"I know. I don't want to yell at the kid.\"\n\n\"Being upset doesn't mean you're going to.\"\n\nHe looked at his hands. \"That guitar was with me when I didn't have much else.\"\n\nI remembered the storage room, the old posters and the letter he had chosen not to destroy. The scratch on a piece of wood wasn't the most painful part of the repair; it was having to trust somebody else with a thing that had once helped him survive.\n\n\"Do you need me to stay here?\" I asked.\n\n\"If you've got time.\"\n\n\"I do. I can sit with you, or I can help Renn sort the inventory. Your choice.\"\n\nHe smiled, half embarrassed. \"Could you just sit?\"\n\nI sat beside him on the low wall. We watched the workshop door open and close while people came through with their own ordinary problems. He didn't have to turn the mistake into a forgiveness speech. Rowan didn't have to become his protégé to make the repayment acceptable.\n\nWhen the first test note sounded from inside, Greg stood so quickly I nearly lost my balance.\n\nDanse appeared holding the guitar.\n\n\"Not finished,\" he said. \"But the joint is holding.\"\n\nGreg let out a breath, laughed at himself, then reached for my hand. I squeezed back.\n\nAfter Rowan left, Danse cleaned the remaining glue residue from the bridge's outer edge before the final finish work. Greg watched with folded arms.\n\n\"You're hovering,\" Danse said.\n\n\"That's my guitar.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"An important guitar.\"\n\n\"I understand.\"\n\nGreg looked surprised.\n\nDanse set the tool down.\n\n\"I once owned equipment that held my whole sense of identity. I know it can be difficult to let someone else repair it.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Yeah. Something like that.\"\n\nThey worked together until evening.\n\nWhen the bridge had set enough for the next stage, Danse placed the guitar in a safe rack and labeled it with the date. Greg signed the work order and left his contact information for the final test.\n\nOutside, the sky was turning pink.\n\n\"How do you feel?\" I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the workshop door.\n\n\"Still disappointed.\"\n\n\"That's allowed.\"\n\n\"And a little better.\"\n\n\"Also allowed.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I used to think if somebody broke something I loved, I had to pretend it was fine or they'd think I didn't love them.\"\n\n\"Now?\"\n\n\"Now I think I can tell them it hurt without turning them into a villain.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe leaned close and kissed me, warm and familiar. The smell of salt and wood glue clung to his jacket.\n\n\"Want to get dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Somewhere with chairs that aren't broken?\"\n\n\"That seems ambitious.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nTwo days later, we returned for the sound test. The guitar played clearly, though the repaired bridge made one low note sound slightly warmer than before.\n\nGreg listened.\n\nThen he played the same little melody again.\n\n\"Huh.\"\n\n\"Is that bad?\" Danse asked.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"No. It's different.\"\n\nHe looked at the scratch along the lower edge.\n\n\"I think I'll leave that part.\"\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Your decision.\"\n\nGreg tucked the guitar into its case.\n\n\"Thanks.\"\n\nThey shook hands.\n\nFor once, neither man seemed to need to prove he had been the most useful person in the room.","word_count":1700},"su-05":{"number":5,"title":"A House with Wheels","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 5 of 40","content":"Greg's van was not technically a house, but it had a kitchen, a bed, and at least three blankets that smelled like sun-warmed fabric.\n\n“It’s a house with wheels,” he said when I pointed this out.\n\n“It’s a van with delusions.”\n\n“Every good home has dreams.”\n\nI was parked outside my studio on a bright Saturday morning, watching Greg attempt to fit a narrow bookshelf through the doorway. The shelf had been left by the previous tenant. One leg was missing. The back panel bowed outward like a person trying not to laugh.\n\n“I’m not sure that thing is safe,” I said.\n\n“It’s been safe for years.”\n\n“That is not the same as being safe now.”\n\n“You sound like the Gems.”\n\n“Should I be worried?”\n\n“Only if they start making a committee.”\n\nHe backed out of the doorway, bookshelf in his arms. The building's hallway was too narrow to turn around, so he shuffled toward the stairs. I followed with a roll of packing paper and the intense conviction that if he fell, I would somehow catch both him and the furniture.\n\nGreg noticed my expression.\n\n“I’m okay,” he said.\n\n“I’m aware.”\n\n“You’re doing the face.”\n\n“What face?”\n\n“The one where you prepare to catch a grown man who has made a poor decision.”\n\n“I have a lot of experience with poor decisions.”\n\n“So do I.”\n\nHe made it down the stairs. The bookshelf did not. It caught on the railing with a loud crack, and one of the remaining legs snapped.\n\nThere was a moment of silence.\n\n“Good news,” Greg said. “It has less wobble now.”\n\nI laughed until I had to sit on the bottom step.\n\nThe bookshelf ended up in the van, where Greg had decided it could become a storage unit for records. I had expected a quick trip to the hardware store, but Greg arrived with a thermos, two sandwiches, and a playlist he claimed was necessary for construction.\n\n“You’re turning this into a road trip,” I said.\n\n“No. I’m turning it into an efficient errand with music.”\n\n“The blanket is suspicious.”\n\n“The blanket is for lumbar support.”\n\n“The pillow?”\n\n“Emotional lumbar support.”\n\nThe van smelled like cedar, coffee, and something sweet I could not identify. A string of small lights ran along the ceiling. A guitar rested in a secure holder near the back. The bed took up one side, covered in mismatched quilts. The other side held shelves of records, tools, books, and objects that seemed to have survived simply because Greg had loved them too much to throw them away.\n\nGreg noticed me examining the guitar in its holder and reached across the bench to check that the neck strap was secure.\n\n\"That one's survived four tours, two spectacularly bad decisions, and one very enthusiastic wash cycle.\"\n\n\"You washed a guitar?\"\n\n\"Not on purpose. Long story. There's a reason I travel with waterproof cases now.\"\n\nHe tapped the latch with a practiced thumb. He was joking, but the care he took with the instrument wasn't a joke. Everything important in the van had a place where a sudden stop wouldn't throw it across the floor.\n\nI pointed at the shelf leaning against the wall. \"So why did you try to carry that down the stairs without help?\"\n\n\"Because sometimes my brilliant ideas arrive ten minutes before my common sense.\"\n\n\"Sometimes?\"\n\n\"Okay, that's fair.\"\n\nHe opened a drawer and took out a small tin of spare screws, each size separated in an old candy box. I had expected chaos. Instead I found a musician's kind of organization: labels for guitar strings, a folded set list, receipts from gigs, and one note written so big it could be read from the driver's seat.\n\nCALL STEVEN.\n\nI pretended I hadn't noticed the tenderness of it until Greg gave the tin to me.\n\n\"You're allowed to tell me I'm being stupid,\" he said, \"as long as you help me find the right screw afterward.\"\n\n\"Does that mean you trust my taste in shelving?\"\n\n\"Not yet. Your taste in music, maybe.\"\n\nHe offered me one of the sandwiches. I chose the less crushed one. He tried to look offended, then traded without complaint. The van was small, but he had made room for a guest to make ordinary choices inside it.\n\nThere was a photograph tucked into the corner of the dashboard. It showed Greg years younger, smiling beside a little boy with enormous hair. I recognized Steven immediately, though I had never met him.\n\n“Your son?” I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the photograph. His expression softened with pride and something more complicated.\n\n“Yeah.”\n\n“He’s cute.”\n\n“He’s a menace.”\n\n“That can be cute.”\n\n“It can also be expensive.”\n\nHe started the van. The engine coughed, complained, and eventually agreed to participate.\n\nAt the hardware store, Greg forgot why he had gone in within three minutes. He remembered when he saw a display of tiny decorative hooks shaped like moons.\n\n“You don’t need those,” I said.\n\n“I need them emotionally.”\n\n“That phrase is becoming a problem.”\n\n“You’re right. I’ll buy two.”\n\nI wandered through aisles of paint and screws while Greg debated the moral character of different kinds of wood glue. When I found him again, he was holding a box of blue paint.\n\n“You said records,” I reminded him.\n\n“The shelf needs a color.”\n\n“It needs legs.”\n\n“Color is more hopeful.”\n\n“Legs are more practical.”\n\n“You’re very focused on practicality for someone who moved to a new city with no housing.”\n\nThe joke was gentle, but I still flinched.\n\nGreg saw it immediately. “I’m sorry.”\n\n“It’s fine.”\n\n“No, it wasn’t. I was teasing you about something tender.”\n\nI looked at the paint can. “You don’t have to apologize every time you miss.”\n\n“I do if I want you to know I noticed.”\n\nThe hardware store around me suddenly seemed too bright. I turned the can in my hands.\n\n“I’m not used to people noticing,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You don’t know everything.”\n\n“No. But I know that.”\n\nHe did not touch me. He simply stood beside the display of paint, giving me enough space to breathe. A teenager pushed a cart past me. A radio played a song about summer.\n\n“Blue is nice,” I said at last.\n\nGreg glanced at the can. “It is.”\n\nThe drive back to the studio took longer because Greg decided to show me the scenic route. The scenic route involved a hill, a view of the water, and a brief argument with a seagull that had landed on the windshield.\n\n“We’re not feeding you,” Greg told it.\n\nThe seagull stared.\n\n“That’s a very judgmental bird,” I said.\n\n“It has known hardship.”\n\n“It stole your capo, didn’t it?”\n\n“Different bird. Same family.”\n\nHe parked near the overlook. The afternoon sun laid a path of gold across the water. Greg turned off the engine but did not get out.\n\n“This is where I bring people when I don’t know how to say something,” he said.\n\nI looked at him. “Are you trying to say something?”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“You’re allowed to use a different word.”\n\nHe smiled, but his hands tightened around the steering wheel.\n\n“I like having you around,” he said. “I know that sounds simple.”\n\n“Simple is not the same as small.”\n\n“I like that you argue with me about chairs and paint. I like that you call when the day is bad and when the day is good. I like that you’re starting to leave things in my van.”\n\nI looked toward the back. My scarf hung from one of the hooks. I had forgotten it there the night before.\n\n“I didn’t know that was a declaration.”\n\n“It might be a scarf.”\n\n“You said it was a house with wheels.”\n\nGreg looked at me then. His eyes were warm, but there was nervousness beneath them.\n\n“I don’t want to assume anything,” he said. “I’ve spent enough of my life mistaking hope for permission.”\n\nThe honesty made the space between us feel suddenly fragile.\n\n“What would you be assuming?”\n\n“That you want this to be more than friendship.”\n\nMy pulse moved into my throat.\n\n“Do you?”\n\nGreg laughed once, softly. “Yes.”\n\nThe word sat between us, clear and unprotected.\n\nI had expected the answer to make me feel trapped. Instead, it made something inside me loosen.\n\n“I do too,” I said.\n\nGreg's eyes closed for a second. He looked relieved enough to make my chest ache.\n\n“Can I kiss you?” he asked.\n\nThe question was quiet. It was also the most certain thing in the world.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe leaned across the space between the seats slowly, giving me every chance to change my mind. His hand rested on the edge of my seat, not my body. When his mouth touched mine, the kiss was gentle enough to feel like an introduction.\n\nI had expected awkwardness. There was some. Greg's nose bumped mine, and he whispered an apology against my mouth. I laughed into the kiss. He smiled, and the smile made it deeper.\n\nHis hand moved to my cheek only after I leaned into it. His thumb brushed my skin. The contact was warm and careful, but not hesitant. Greg kissed me like somebody who had learned that tenderness could be fierce.\n\nWhen I pulled back, my foreheads touched.\n\n“That was better than the chair,” I murmured.\n\n“I’m going to be offended if you compare all our kisses to furniture.”\n\n“You started a theme.”\n\n“I can improve.”\n\nThe next kiss was slower. The van rocked slightly when I shifted closer. Somewhere outside, the seagull cried as if filing a complaint.\n\nGreg drew back first, breathing hard enough to make me smile.\n\n“I should probably mention,” he said, “that the van has a very bad privacy curtain.”\n\n“You have a privacy curtain?”\n\n“I have a curtain with aspirations.”\n\nI laughed, and Greg's gaze dropped briefly to my mouth.\n\nThe warmth between us changed. It did not become a demand. It became an invitation, one that waited for my answer.\n\nI kissed him again. His hand slid from my cheek to the back of my neck, holding me close without pulling. The world outside reduced itself to water, sunlight, and the low sound he made when my fingers curled into his shirt.\n\nWhen the windows began to fog, Greg rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“We can stop,” he whispered.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Or we can go somewhere less public.”\n\n“I know that too.”\n\nThe choice was mine. The warmth in his eyes told me he would be happy either way, as long as I was choosing freely.\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\n“Take me home,” I said.\n\nGreg's smile was soft and almost disbelieving. “Okay.”\n\nHe drove back through Beach City with one hand on the wheel and the other intertwined with mine. The blue paint sat between my feet. The broken bookshelf waited in the back. My scarf hung from the moon-shaped hook.\n\nThe van was still not technically a house.\n\nBut when Greg parked outside my building and walked me upstairs, when he kissed me again beneath the crooked hallway light, when the lock turned and the door closed behind me, home stopped feeling like a place I had to earn.\n\nThe rest of the afternoon belonged to the two of us, the ocean, and easy conversation that did not ask either of us to perform for anyone.","word_count":1893},"su-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Shape of Sorry","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 27 of 40","content":"The pasta had turned into a single damp opinion about disappointment.\n\nI'd cooked it because Greg said he'd be home for dinner. I had even put the little battery candles on the table, since I knew we'd both end up knocking over real ones. At eight, the food was cold. At nine, I stopped looking at the clock and started doing the dishes.\n\nAt ten, he came through the door with wet flowers and a broken umbrella.\n\n\"Okay,\" he said. \"I have a really bad explanation.\"\n\n\"That makes two of us. I have really bad pasta.\"\n\nHe tried to laugh. I couldn't.\n\nGreg lowered the flowers.\n\nHe had been helping a neighbor whose van wouldn't start. One repair had become another, then a trip to find the right belt, then a long drive back. The neighbor was safe. Greg had his phone. He just hadn't called because every time he looked up, he thought he'd be done in five more minutes.\n\n\"Did you think I wouldn't notice?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I thought I could still make it.\"\n\n\"At ten?\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I know.\"\n\nI didn't want to be cruel. I also didn't want to turn my hurt into a little smile that would teach him there was no cost to letting me wait.\n\n\"Can we sit down?\" I asked.\n\nHe took the chair opposite me. The flowers dripped onto the table. He put them in the sink, then sat again without asking whether that made everything better.\n\n\"I was excited about tonight,\" I said. \"Not because you were supposed to prove something. Just because I'd saved the evening for us.\"\n\n\"I was excited too.\"\n\n\"Then why was I the last person you thought to tell?\"\n\nHe looked at his hands.\n\n\"Because that neighbor was right there asking me for help,\" he said. \"You weren't. And I thought if I called, I'd have to say I was choosing something else instead of you.\"\n\n\"Not calling was a choice too.\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nThe apology came slowly. Not dramatic, not lyrical. He said he'd left me waiting, that I had every reason to be upset, and that he wouldn't make me responsible for reassuring him while he apologized.\n\nI nearly cried at that. I hated how much a plain sentence could matter.\n\n\"Are you asking me to forgive you now?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I'd like it eventually. But right now I'm trying to listen.\"\n\nWe reheated what was left of the pasta and added a little water until it was at least edible. Greg offered to go out and buy dinner instead. I said no. Buying an expensive meal wasn't the same as showing up.\n\nWhile we waited for the water to heat, Greg looked at the flowers he'd brought. The stems were bent and one bloom had lost half its petals. He started to put them into a vase, then stopped.\n\n\"I bought these because I thought maybe it'd make you less mad.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"That's not fair to you.\"\n\n\"No. But they can still be flowers.\"\n\nHe nodded. He found a chipped jar and filled it with clean water, then set the bouquet on the windowsill without expecting me to admire it. I watched him take the quiet answer seriously.\n\n\"Do you think I'm always going to mess up like this?\" he asked.\n\n\"I think we're both going to make mistakes. I don't think we have to keep making the same ones.\"\n\nHe sat down opposite me. His hair was damp from the rain, and there was a little smear of grease along his wrist where he'd helped with the neighbor's van.\n\n\"I thought if I kept helping,\" he said, \"you'd be proud of me.\"\n\n\"I'm proud that you helped. I'm upset you left me waiting without telling me. Those are two different things.\"\n\nHe let out a breath. \"I keep wanting them to cancel each other.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nGreg ran his finger along the edge of the table where a strip of tape had come loose.\n\n\"When I was playing gigs all the time, I'd get asked to do one more song,\" he said. \"Then one more. You say yes enough and suddenly the whole night belongs to the room instead of to you.\"\n\n\"That sounds familiar.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I thought helping with the van was different because it wasn't music. But I did the same thing. I kept thinking there'd be an easy last step.\"\n\n\"Did you ever find one?\"\n\nHe looked at the overcooked pasta and made a face. \"Usually about three hours late, after the amps were packed and everybody was mad.\"\n\nI wanted to laugh. I also wanted him to remember how lonely the evening had been.\n\n\"Greg, I'm not asking you to stop being the person who helps.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Are you sure? Because you keep looking like I'm about to take your license away.\"\n\nHe put on a wounded expression. \"My license to make terrible decisions?\"\n\n\"That's the one.\"\n\n\"Expired years ago.\"\n\nThis time I did laugh. He reached for my hand, then stopped, giving me the choice to close the distance. I did, but I left the ruined dinner between us as evidence. A joke could make it easier to breathe; it couldn't erase four hours of waiting.\n\n\"Okay,\" he said, rubbing his thumb over my knuckles. \"Let's figure out the boring practical thing that keeps me from doing it again.\"\n\nThat sounded like him. Not a polished promise. A real person offering to change a habit while the sauce congealed.\n\nWe wrote the revised plan on the back of the grocery list. I didn't want a promise that he'd never help someone again. I wanted him to notice when another request would cost somebody else the time he'd already agreed to give.\n\n\"What if I'm in the middle of a repair and my hands are full?\" he asked.\n\n\"Then send a message when it's safe. Tell me as soon as you reasonably can. Don't wait because you think you're nearly done.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"And I can say that I'm disappointed without you making it a verdict on whether we're right for each other.\"\n\nHe looked down at the paper. \"Yeah. I need to work on that.\"\n\nThe pasta was still terrible. We ate it anyway. By the end of dinner, we'd stopped trying to find one sentence that would make everything all right.\n\nGreg reached toward my hand, paused and asked.\n\n\"Can I?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe held it across the table. There was no need to kiss yet, and for the first time that evening neither of us was trying to hurry the other person into feeling better.\n\nWhile the sauce warmed, we made a practical plan. If he accepted a favor that would affect our agreed time, he'd send a message. If he couldn't safely stop to call, he'd tell me as soon as he could. If he needed to cancel, we'd decide whether to reschedule instead of leaving the evening hanging. I promised not to treat every late arrival as a verdict on how much he loved me.\n\n\"I have other partners, Greg,\" I said, not because he had forgotten but because it mattered to the plan. \"I've been clear that I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I keep a calendar because the people I love shouldn't have to guess whether I'm coming. You deserve that from me too.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"I don't want to be the guy who makes plans impossible.\"\n\n\"Then start with one plan you can keep.\"\n\n\"Tomorrow breakfast?\"\n\n\"I have an early visit tomorrow.\"\n\nHe smiled ruefully. \"Okay. See, this is why I should have asked.\"\n\n\"Friday evening?\"\n\n\"Friday. I'll put it in writing.\"\n\nHe took a receipt from his pocket and wrote our agreed time on the back. Then he paused. \"Actually, I'll put it somewhere I won't accidentally wash it.\"\n\n\"The bar is rising.\"\n\nHe laughed for real.\n\nThe next morning a neighbor came by the car wash asking whether Greg could look at a faulty engine. He said he could inspect it after his booked work, not immediately. The neighbor went elsewhere for the emergency repair. Nobody died of disappointment.\n\nBy Wednesday, the neighbor had sent Greg three messages asking whether he'd reconsider working on the van. Greg showed me the phone with an expression somewhere between guilt and dread.\n\n“I could squeeze it in,” he said.\n\n“Would you want to?”\n\nHe opened his mouth and stopped. The question seemed to have surprised him more than the requests.\n\n“I'd like to help,” he said at last. “But I'm already booked at the car wash. And I promised Steven we'd talk on Thursday. If I promise one more thing, somebody loses.”\n\n“Then tell the neighbor what you can actually offer.”\n\nHe typed a message, erased it, typed another, and finally handed me the phone.\n\n“Too rude?”\n\nIt said that he couldn't do an emergency repair, but could recommend a mechanic with an opening. It was kind and clear. The neighbor replied with a thumbs-up and a thank-you.\n\nGreg looked offended by how simple it had been. “No dramatic argument. No speech about how I'm abandoning everybody.”\n\n“You'll have to write a song about the disappointment.”\n\n“I've got a title. Ballad of the Responsible Calendar. Nobody's buying tickets.”\n\nOn Thursday Steven called later than planned. Greg let the conversation run because his son needed him. He sent me a message before dinner to say he'd be a little late stopping by. The message wasn't poetic, but I felt myself loosen when it appeared.\n\nI liked that his apology wasn't perfect. It was becoming a habit of choosing a different action before the hurt could repeat.\n\nOn Friday, Greg arrived on time with a bag of vegetables and a determination not to touch the stove without instructions. The meal was better. We laughed about the first disaster, but we didn't pretend it hadn't hurt.\n\nWhen dinner was done, he reached across the table.\n\n\"Want me to hold your hand?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe sat there until I was the one who leaned in for a kiss. It was warm, familiar, and still had a little awkwardness in it. We closed the curtains later, with no audience and no need to perform an apology beyond what we'd actually changed.\n\nThe broken umbrella stayed by the door for a week. Then Greg fixed it, tested it in the rain, and gave it back to the neighbor he'd borrowed it from.\n\nI liked that he remembered a promise small enough not to become a song.","word_count":1754},"su-45":{"number":45,"title":"A Better Kind of Jealousy","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass three","content":"The rumor reached Greg before I got back from Brasswatch.\n\nI found him sitting on the hood of the van with a paper cup of coffee and the look he wore when he was trying to decide whether a joke would make something less painful.\n\n\"Hey,\" he said.\n\n\"Hey.\"\n\nHe smiled too quickly.\n\n\"Busy day?\"\n\n\"Very. Danse finally got the workshop door working without a lock you need a ladder to reach.\"\n\n\"That's good.\"\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\nHe stared into his coffee.\n\n\"Nothing. Just heard a weird thing.\"\n\nI waited.\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"Somebody said you're planning to move into Brasswatch permanently. That you and Danse are making some kind of big announcement. That I'm basically the guy you visit when there's music.\"\n\nI blinked.\n\n\"Who said that?\"\n\n\"Does it matter?\"\n\n\"It matters that someone is inventing a story about my life.\"\n\nGreg tried to laugh.\n\n\"Yeah, well, people like a good story.\"\n\n\"That doesn't make it true.\"\n\nHe shrugged, still looking at the cup.\n\nI sat beside him, close enough that our shoulders touched but not so close that he couldn't move away.\n\n\"Do you believe it?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\" He hesitated. \"Not exactly.\"\n\n\"Then what is it?\"\n\nHe rubbed the heel of his hand against his forehead.\n\n\"I know what we agreed. I know you're ethically non-monogamous, that you prefer polyamory, and that none of this means you're promising to be exclusive with me. I've said yes to being part of your life because I want to be here.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But sometimes I hear people talk like there's a scoreboard, and I start wondering if I'm losing.\"\n\nThe words were plain enough to make my chest tighten.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I'm not proud of that.\"\n\n\"You don't have to be ashamed of feeling insecure.\"\n\n\"I don't want to turn into somebody who needs to know where you are every minute.\"\n\n\"Then don't. Tell me what you're afraid of.\"\n\nHe took a long breath.\n\n\"That one day you'll find somebody whose life fits yours better. Somebody who can build things, fight monsters, fix doors, whatever. And I'll be the guy who makes you laugh until you don't need that anymore.\"\n\nI looked at his hands, rough from strings and work. I thought about the fundraiser, the way he'd learned to say no to a song that hurt him, and the small note he'd left on the dashboard reminding himself not to promise the universe on my behalf.\n\n\"You're not a consolation prize,\" I said.\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"Wasn't planning to enter a competition.\"\n\n\"Good. Because there isn't one.\"\n\nHe took the empty coffee lid off the cup and put it back on again.\n\n\"Can I tell you the really embarrassing part?\" he asked.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"I heard the rumor and immediately started rehearsing what I'd say if you told me it was true. Like an audition for a conversation you hadn't agreed to have.\"\n\n\"What was your opening line?\"\n\n\"Terrible. I was gonna say I should write a sad song about you leaving.\"\n\nI raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"Then I realized you'd make fun of the chorus.\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\nHe laughed, but the sound faded when he looked toward the van. I knew he could play a room full of people without blinking. The idea of asking one person whether he mattered still made his hands nervous.\n\n\"Sometimes I worry that if I make space for all your other relationships, I'll forget to ask for any of my own,\" he said.\n\n\"Then ask. I can't promise yes to every plan. I can promise I won't treat the question as a competition.\"\n\nHe rolled the paper cup between his palms. \"Okay. Can I ask you to hear my new song before anybody else does?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even if it's embarrassing?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nHe looked relieved, then horrified at having revealed that much. \"Uh, warning: it's about a bass line that sounds like a refrigerator.\"\n\nI bumped his shoulder with mine. He kissed my temple after I leaned toward him, still smelling of coffee and the salt air. No rumor, no council, and no other partner had been asked to certify what we felt. He could make his request himself.\n\nHe took another breath.\n\n\"I know jealousy doesn't mean somebody's done something wrong.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it tells us we need something. Time. Reassurance. A clearer plan.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Can I ask for a clearer plan?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe went inside the little house near the beach and sat at the kitchen table. Greg set the coffee aside and took out a pencil. I brought my notebook.\n\nWe talked about what was actually changing.\n\nI was helping Danse with the workshop and spending more time at Brasswatch during repairs. That didn't mean I was moving there. Iroh's teahouse needed help after the roof leak. Riverstone's bridge working group had a schedule I wanted to respect. I still wanted time with Greg in Beach City and at Starwake Pier.\n\n\"You could have told me the Brasswatch work was taking more hours,\" he said.\n\n\"I thought I had.\"\n\n\"You told me about the door. Not about the whole week.\"\n\nI winced.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'm not asking you to report to me. I just want to know when our plans are likely to change.\"\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nI opened my calendar and showed Greg the week he was worried about. I'd marked Brasswatch as a series of half-days because Danse had asked for help reviewing a set of workshop doors. The work had run into a second week when the locking mechanism failed its safety test.\n\n\"That's why I kept talking about the door,\" I said. \"It was the part I thought would be finished.\"\n\nGreg leaned closer, but he didn't reach for the paper until I handed it to him.\n\n\"Did you tell Danse you had plans with me?\"\n\n\"Yes. He knew the days I'd said I was free. I should've told both of you when the work shifted.\"\n\nHe considered that for a moment.\n\n\"Okay. I'm not mad that you spent time with him. I'm mad that I kept finding out about the changes from other people.\"\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nI felt embarrassed enough to make a joke and decided not to. The rumor had hurt because it found a real gap in our communication and filled it with the cruelest story available.\n\n\"Would you like to tell me which parts of your life you don't want me to ask about?\" I said.\n\nHe looked puzzled. \"Like what?\"\n\n\"I ask about your shows, your family, when you miss Rose. But maybe there are things you'd rather bring up yourself.\"\n\nGreg rubbed the back of his neck. \"I don't want to hide stuff. I just don't want to feel like every bad mood is an interview.\"\n\n\"Then tell me when you're not ready.\"\n\n\"Will you believe me?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe reached across the seat and held my hand for a moment.\n\n\"Okay. Then I want us to be allowed to miss each other without it becoming evidence that polyamory doesn't work.\"\n\nThat landed hard. We were both entitled to feelings without appointing them judges.\n\n\"I want that too,\" I said.\n\nGreg leaned closer and kissed my cheek. It was so tentative I almost missed the question inside it. I turned toward him.\n\n\"Can I kiss you properly?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe kissed slowly until the coffee went cold. When we drew apart, his hair was more untidy than before and I had forgotten what I'd intended to say next.\n\n\"Now,\" he said, \"we can do the terrifying part.\"\n\n\"The calendar?\"\n\n\"The calendar.\"\n\nWe checked the calendar.\n\nGreg wanted a regular evening to play music together, even if I was terrible at it. I wanted one day a month where we could go somewhere without turning the outing into a community service project. He needed the freedom to visit Steven without assuming I'd always come along. I needed him to be able to tell me when gossip hurt instead of pretending it was funny.\n\n\"That last one sounds hard,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"I can try.\"\n\n\"That's all I'm asking.\"\n\nHe wrote something in the margin.\n\nI leaned over.\n\n\"What's that?\"\n\n\"Official Greg Rule Number One.\"\n\nI read it.\n\nDON'T LET STRANGERS WRITE YOUR LOVE SONGS.\n\nI laughed.\n\n\"That's a good rule.\"\n\n\"Number Two is probably don't drink three cups of coffee before discussing feelings.\"\n\n\"Also good.\"\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"Can I ask one more thing?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"If you ever do make a big change, will you tell me yourself?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not because you own the decision. Because you matter to me.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nWe sat for a while without talking. The house smelled of coffee and salt air. A gull called outside, then another answered. Somewhere down the street, a car door slammed.\n\nGreg reached for my hand.\n\n\"I love you,\" he said.\n\n\"I love you too.\"\n\nHe smiled, slow and genuine.\n\nThen he leaned forward, stopped just short of my face, and raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"Am I allowed to kiss you, or do I need to schedule it?\"\n\n\"You can kiss me.\"\n\n\"Good. I was running out of paperwork.\"\n\nHis kiss was warm and a little clumsy. I laughed against his mouth. He pulled back, grinning.\n\n\"There. Much better than coffee.\"\n\n\"Depends on the coffee.\"\n\n\"Rude.\"\n\nI kissed him again.\n\nThe rest of the afternoon belonged to an ordinary day together. We washed the dishes, fixed a loose hinge on a cupboard, and took a walk along the boardwalk. Greg stopped to talk to a musician he knew, and I bought a small packet of sea-salt candy from a shop near the pier.\n\nWe didn't solve jealousy forever.\n\nAt dinner, Greg admitted he still felt a little nervous about the rumor.\n\nI told him he could ask me again if he needed reassurance, and I would answer honestly rather than make him feel foolish.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Then I'm going to ask something very important.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Are you staying for the movie?\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Great. Because I've already made popcorn.\"\n\nLater, when the lights were low and the movie had become background noise, he rested his head against my shoulder. I brushed my fingers through his hair, and he took my hand without looking away from the screen.\n\nOutside, the tide moved in and out along Beach City.\n\nIt had always done that.\n\nIt didn't have to choose one shore forever to keep returning.","word_count":1742},"su-06":{"number":6,"title":"Do Not Make Me Guess","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 6 of 40","content":"In the morning, Greg made coffee as though nothing in the world had changed.\n\nThe coffee maker made a noise like an angry lawn mower, and Greg slapped the side of it with the affectionate impatience of a man who had kept cheap equipment working for years.\n\n“Morning, you beautiful disaster,” he told the machine.\n\n“Are you talking to it or me?”\n\n“Both. One of you owes me a new filter.”\n\nThe joke landed, but there was something underneath it. Last night had happened. I could still feel the impression of his hand at my waist, the almost unbearable tenderness of being wanted without being hurried. In daylight, my borrowed sweatpants on his legs looked ridiculous, and I was suddenly afraid that our happiness would collapse under the weight of ordinary breakfast.\n\nGreg saw me looking at the clothes.\n\n“Just so we're clear, I'm returning these,” he said. “Unless you wanna start a collection of very fashionable ankle pants.”\n\n“Keep them.”\n\nHe set the mug down. “Hey. Did you just give me your only comfortable sweatpants because you think I need a souvenir?”\n\nI hesitated.\n\n“Okay,” he said, softer. “Then let's not make that the rule. I'll bring them back after I wash them.”\n\nHe leaned against the counter rather than approaching me. It gave me space to laugh, to look at him, to decide what I wanted to do. I crossed the kitchen and caught his ridiculous shirt in my fingers.\n\n“Can I kiss you before the machine kills us both?”\n\n“Please. I need something to remember me by.”\n\nHis mouth was warm with coffee, and he laughed when I nearly knocked over the sugar jar. The second kiss was less graceful. I liked it better.\n\nHe stood in my kitchenette wearing the same T-shirt from yesterday and a pair of borrowed sweatpants that were too short in the legs. His hair was sleep-tousled. One hand held a mug; the other tried to operate the ancient coffee maker without making eye contact with the machine.\n\n“It’s judging me,” he said.\n\nI was still in bed, wrapped in the blankets that had seemed perfectly ordinary before the night became something else. “It knows what you did.”\n\nGreg looked over his shoulder. “I made coffee.”\n\n“That’s not what I meant.”\n\n“Oh.” His ears turned pink. “Right.”\n\nThe night returned in fragments: the careful questions, my yes, the way Greg had kissed me as if there were nowhere else he needed to be. There had been laughter, warmth, and an afternoon that ended with the two of us tangled in blankets while the bakery below prepared for the next day.\n\nNothing about it had felt rushed. Nothing had felt taken.\n\nStill, morning had a way of making every decision look larger.\n\nGreg carried two mugs to the bed and sat on the edge, leaving enough room for me to decide whether to close it. I accepted mine. The coffee was sweet and slightly too strong.\n\n“I’m sorry,” he said.\n\nI blinked. “For the coffee?”\n\n“No.” He looked at the mug. “For not asking whether you wanted me to stay.”\n\nI held the coffee between both hands. “You didn’t have to ask.”\n\n“I know. But I should have.”\n\nThe admission was disarming. I had expected him to be cheerful, maybe embarrassed, perhaps distant. I had not expected him to offer an apology before I knew me needed one.\n\n“I wanted you to stay,” I said.\n\nGreg looked up.\n\n“I still want you here,” I continued. “I just don’t know what this is supposed to look like.”\n\n“Neither do I.”\n\n“You seem like somebody who would know.”\n\n“I look very confident from a distance.”\n\n“That’s because your hair has authority.”\n\nHe smiled, then sobered. “We can decide one piece at a time.”\n\n“What piece are we deciding now?”\n\n“Whether you want breakfast.”\n\nThe question made me laugh. Greg looked pleased, but he did not use the moment to avoid the larger conversation.\n\n“Yes,” I said. “I want breakfast.”\n\n“Great. I can make pancakes.”\n\n“Do you know how?”\n\n“I know several shapes.”\n\nHe got up. The room felt different when he moved away, emptier in a way that was not yet painful but could become so if I stared at it.\n\n“Greg.”\n\nHe turned.\n\n“Are you going to disappear?”\n\nThe question came out smaller than I intended.\n\nHe set the mug down. “No.”\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“I know what I’m choosing.”\n\n“People choose things and then change their minds.”\n\n“They do.”\n\n“You might.”\n\n“I might.” He walked back to the bed and crouched so I was looking at each other. “If I change my mind, I’ll tell you. I won’t punish you with silence and call it kindness.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\n“And if you change yours,” he said, “you tell me. You don’t have to stay because we kissed. You don’t owe me a relationship because I bought a chair.”\n\n“The chair was very nice.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“I might owe you a little.”\n\n“A cinnamon roll would settle the debt.”\n\nThe tension loosened enough for me to breathe.\n\nGreg stood again and began making pancakes. He hummed while he worked, the melody slipping in and out of tune. I watched his shoulders move beneath the shirt, the easy confidence of his hands, the way he checked the pan before adding batter.\n\nThe pancake in the middle of the pan had acquired a shape that looked remarkably like a badly tuned guitar.\n\n\"That's either breakfast or my first album cover,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Your first album cover?\"\n\n\"Less flattering. Worse lighting.\"\n\nHe slid the pancake onto a plate, shook his head at its uneven edges, and pushed the best one toward me. I took the crooked one instead. It tasted of cinnamon and something he had almost certainly put in without measuring.\n\n\"Hey,\" he said after a minute. \"We should talk about what last night means before I accidentally start acting like I know.\"\n\nI set down my fork. \"That's a good idea.\"\n\n\"Okay. For starters, you don't owe me a whole future because we had a good night.\"\n\n\"I know.\" The words were easy to say and less easy to believe. \"And I want to be clear that I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I'm not asking you to share every part of your life with anybody else, but I won't pretend I only ever love one person.\"\n\nGreg nodded slowly. \"Thanks for telling me straight. I might have questions. Maybe some really dumb ones.\"\n\n\"Ask.\"\n\n\"Does this mean I can still make you awful pancakes without taking a ticket and getting in line?\"\n\nI laughed hard enough to choke on my coffee. \"Yes, Greg.\"\n\n\"Great. I have a competitive advantage in awful pancakes.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand across the table and waited for me to meet him halfway. I did. We hadn't made a schedule or promised the world, but we had decided neither one of us would have to guess what the other meant by wanting to see them again.\n\n“You’re staring,” he said without turning.\n\n“You’re making breakfast in my kitchen.”\n\n“That’s not a defense.”\n\n“It’s my best one.”\n\n“I accept.”\n\nThe first pancake was too pale. The second was shaped vaguely like a crescent moon. The third landed halfway on the plate.\n\n“You said several shapes,” I reminded him.\n\n“I didn’t say they were edible shapes.”\n\nI ate all three.\n\nAfter breakfast, Greg washed the dishes. He did not put my things away without asking. When he picked up a mug, he paused and looked at me.\n\n“Cabinet or counter?”\n\n“Counter.”\n\n“Good to know.”\n\nThe tiny question touched me more than it should have. I had lived with people who moved through my space as if my preferences were clutter. Greg treated a mug like a negotiation.\n\nWhen he finished, he dried his hands on a towel and faced me.\n\n“There’s something I want to say.”\n\n“That sounds serious.”\n\n“It is, but not bad serious.”\n\nHe leaned against the counter. “I’m attracted to you. I like you. I like what happened yesterday, and I would like it to happen again. But I don’t want you to feel like you have to be easy for me. You can ask for space. You can say no. You can change the plan halfway through.”\n\n“You’re giving me a lot of instructions for someone who just burned a pancake.”\n\n“I contain contradictions.”\n\n“So do I.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nThe warmth returned to his eyes.\n\nI stepped closer. “What if I don’t know what I want until I’m already doing it?”\n\n“Then we check in while we’re doing it.”\n\n“What if I say yes and then panic?”\n\n“Then we stop and panic together, or separately, depending on what you want.”\n\n“What if I don’t say anything?”\n\n“Then I ask.”\n\nI was close enough now to smell coffee and clean soap. Greg did not reach for me.\n\n“What if I want you to kiss me?”\n\n“Then you can tell me.”\n\n“You could ask.”\n\nHis smile was gentle. “Do you want me to?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Can I kiss you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe kiss was not like yesterday. It did not carry the nervous rush of a first crossing. It was slower, more knowing. Greg's hand settled at my waist only after I touched his shoulder. When I leaned back against the counter, he followed without pressing me there.\n\n“Still okay?” he whispered.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Tell me if that changes.”\n\n“I will.”\n\nHe kissed the corner of my mouth. Then my cheek. Then the place beneath my ear that made my breath catch.\n\n“Still okay?”\n\nI pulled him closer by the front of his shirt. “Ask me one more time and I’m going to start charging you for repetition.”\n\n“I can pay in muffins.”\n\n“Then continue.”\n\nThe morning unfolded in a softer rhythm. There were kisses and laughter, hands resting where they were welcome, and a moment when Greg's forehead pressed against mine while both of us tried to remember how breathing worked.\n\nWe kept the morning soft with kisses, laughter, and a long conversation about what each of us wanted next.\n\nLater, after we had talked ourselves tired, I woke from a nap with Greg asleep beside me. His arm lay across the mattress, not holding me, only near enough that I could reach it. Sunlight moved across the wall.\n\nI had expected regret. Instead, I felt the quiet shock of being safe after wanting something.\n\nGreg woke when I shifted. “Hey.”\n\n“Hey.”\n\n“Did I talk in my sleep?”\n\n“You apologized to the pancakes.”\n\n“They were under a lot of pressure.”\n\nI turned toward him. “What happens now?”\n\nHe looked at the ceiling. “We keep talking. We keep asking. We see if we like each other when we’re tired and annoying.”\n\n“You’re already annoying.”\n\n“Excellent. We’re ahead of schedule.”\n\nI smiled, but the question remained.\n\n“Do you want to call this dating?” I asked.\n\nGreg considered me. “Do you?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“That’s okay.”\n\n“You’re not going to make me guess?”\n\n“No.” He touched the back of my hand. “I want to date you. If you want to date me. If you don’t, I still want to be someone you can call. But I’m not going to pretend I only want friendship if that isn’t true.”\n\nHis directness made my heart beat harder.\n\n“I want to date you,” I said.\n\n“Okay.”\n\n“That’s all?”\n\n“I’m trying to remain calm.”\n\n“You’re failing.”\n\n“I’m doing my best.”\n\nHe kissed my hand. The gesture was almost old-fashioned, and yet it felt less like a performance than a promise to keep showing up.\n\nThat evening, I walked with him to the boardwalk. Greg carried the new chair. I carried the guitar case. The division of weight was not equal, but it was agreed upon. When I reached the sea wall, he set the chair down and waited for me to sit before taking the space beside me.\n\n“I’m going to write a song about this,” he said.\n\n“About the chair?”\n\n“About the way you keep making me ask.”\n\n“That sounds threatening.”\n\n“It’s affectionate.”\n\nThe sunset spread pink and orange across the water. Greg's shoulder rested against mine.\n\n“You know,” I said, “you can tell me what you want too.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“You keep making space for me,” I continued. “But you’re allowed to take up some.”\n\nThe expression on his face was so tender that I nearly looked away.\n\n“I want to,” he said.\n\n“Then do.”\n\nGreg shifted closer. His hand found mine between the chairs.\n\n“I want to be here,” he said. “With you. I want to learn what your good days look like. I want to make breakfast badly and hear you complain about my furniture. I want to know whether you ever stop looking at exits.”\n\n“Do you want the honest answer?”\n\n“Always.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then I’ll sit where you can see the door.”\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe ocean did not promise anything. The future remained uncertain. But Greg was beside me, asking instead of assuming, making room instead of taking it.\n\nFor that evening, it was enough.","word_count":2177},"su-20":{"number":20,"title":"Everything I Need to Say","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 20 of 40","content":"The song was called Everything I Need to Say.\n\nGreg played it at the boardwalk on the first warm evening of spring. The new chair had been replaced by two folding chairs and a small table between them. The table held tea, coffee, and a plate of pastries that he insisted were not a bribe.\n\n“They’re a celebration,” he said.\n\n“Of what?”\n\n“The fact that I have written a song without mentioning a chair.”\n\n“That is worthy of recognition.”\n\nThe crowd gathered as the sun lowered. Mara sat near the front. Steven stood beside the railing, smiling at his father. Lila was there too, wearing the red jacket, and when she saw me she hugged me with permission first.\n\n“I’m glad you came,” she said.\n\n“I’m glad you’re not a villain.”\n\nShe laughed. “Greg told me you were funny.”\n\n“He tells everyone my secrets.”\n\n“Only the flattering ones,” Greg called from the stage.\n\n“You’re going to regret that,” I called back.\n\n“I regret most things eventually.”\n\nThe show began with familiar songs. Greg played about the ocean, the car wash, and a raccoon with suspiciously good rhythm. He made the crowd laugh. He made them quiet.\n\nThen he set the old guitar pick on the edge of the microphone stand.\n\n“This next one is new,” he said. “It’s about a person who arrived in Beach City with a suitcase, a terrible motel, and no plan.”\n\nI felt every eye turn toward me.\n\nGreg caught my gaze. “They said I could sing it.”\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe began.\n\nThe melody was gentle, built from the chords he had played the night I met. It carried the sea without imitating it. The first verse spoke of arriving with nowhere to put my keys. The second spoke of a chair, a cinnamon roll, and the difference between an offer and a chain.\n\nThe chorus was simple.\n\nI am not a place I escaped to.\n\nI am the place I choose.\n\nThe words landed in the open air. Greg sang them without turning me into a symbol. He sang about my jokes, my fear, my boundaries, the way I looked at exits and still stayed for the song.\n\nWhen he reached the last verse, he held out one hand.\n\nI stood and walked to the stage.\n\n“You don’t have to sing,” he murmured.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you want to?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe shifted the microphone toward me. My shoulder touched his. The crowd blurred.\n\nThe final chorus belonged to both of us.\n\nMy voice was not perfect. Neither was his. Together, the song became something larger than either of us, a conversation carried by strings and breath.\n\nWhen it ended, the applause rose like a wave.\n\nGreg bowed, then offered me his hand. I took it.\n\nAfter the show, Steven hugged us both at once.\n\n“That was beautiful,” he said.\n\n“Your dad was showing off,” I replied.\n\n“I know. He does that when he has feelings.”\n\nGreg put a hand over his heart. “I am being attacked by my family.”\n\nMara brought me a paper plate. “Eat before he writes another verse.”\n\nThe night moved around me. People congratulated Greg. A child asked me to sing the song again. Lila told me the bridge was good and looked surprised when Greg admitted she had helped him fix it.\n\n“You let her edit your song?” I asked.\n\n“She has excellent instincts.”\n\n“I’m going to become jealous of the bridge.”\n\n“The bridge is very committed to us.”\n\nLater, after everyone left, I and Greg walked to the water. The boardwalk lights reflected across the dark surface.\n\n“You did it,” he said.\n\n“I sang in public.”\n\n“You chose to be seen.”\n\n“Don’t make it a lesson.”\n\n“I’m not.” He stopped near the edge of the pier. “I’m making it a memory.”\n\nI leaned against the railing. “The song was beautiful.”\n\n“You’re biased.”\n\n“I’m also a professional.”\n\nGreg laughed. “You’ve been waiting to use that.”\n\n“For months.”\n\nThe ocean moved beneath me.\n\n“I love you,” Greg said.\n\n“I love you too.”\n\n“I want to ask you something.”\n\n“Is it a question?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then ask.”\n\nHe took a small box from his jacket. My heart stopped.\n\n“It’s not a ring,” he said quickly. “I know what your face is doing.”\n\n“My face is doing nothing.”\n\n“Your face is preparing for a dramatic escape.”\n\n“Maybe I’m curious.”\n\nGreg watched my expression and rubbed the back of his neck.\n\n\"Okay, I'm gonna make this less mysterious,\" he said. \"The thing in the box isn't a dramatic life-changing contract.\"\n\n\"That is exactly what a person says before producing a dramatic life-changing contract.\"\n\n\"Fair. But hear me out.\"\n\nSteven was standing a few steps away, talking to the sound technician. Greg glanced toward him with a little smile before turning back.\n\n\"When I was younger, I thought letting somebody into my space meant giving up the freedom to be me,\" he said. \"Then I built an entire life out of a van and a guitar. Turns out you can still make room.\"\n\n\"Provided your van has more than one seat.\"\n\n\"And the passenger seat doesn't contain sixty-seven loose guitar picks.\"\n\n\"Sixty-eight.\"\n\nHe looked appalled. \"You counted?\"\n\n\"I keep finding them in my pockets.\"\n\nHe laughed, then grew quiet. I could tell the joke had given him enough courage to ask a real question.\n\n\"If you wanted to leave a book or a sweater in there, would that feel okay? Not moving in. Not declaring anything. Just knowing you don't have to carry everything every time.\"\n\nI touched his wrist. \"I would like that. And you can ask me the same kind of question about my place.\"\n\nHe swallowed. The ordinary little request mattered more than a speech about forever. I didn't stop loving other people because Greg had offered me a place to set down a sweater. I did start imagining what it might be like to reach for one and find it waiting.\n\nHe opened the box. Inside was a key on a simple metal ring, engraved with a tiny star.\n\n“You already have a key to my apartment,” I said.\n\n“I know. This is for the van.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“I want you to have access,” he said. “Not because you owe me visits. Not because I expect you to use it. Because it is part of my life, and I want you to be able to enter without waiting outside.”\n\nMy fingers trembled as I took the key.\n\n“You’re sure?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What if I lose it?”\n\n“We find it again.”\n\n“What if I stop using it?”\n\n“Then it stays a key.”\n\nThe answer made me smile through the tears gathering in my eyes.\n\n“I’ll take it,” I said.\n\nGreg closed my fingers around it.\n\n“Can I kiss you?”\n\n“You wrote a whole song and still have to ask?”\n\n“Always.”\n\nI kissed him beneath the boardwalk lights. His hands held my waist. The world narrowed to warmth, salt air, and the quiet certainty that no part of this required me to disappear.\n\nWhen I returned to the apartment, the door opened with my key. Greg followed with his.\n\nThe living room still had boxes in one corner. The bed was finally assembled. The record player worked. My photograph stood beside the lamp, and the new key joined the others in the dish.\n\nGreg turned on a record.\n\nI danced in the kitchen.\n\nThe song ended, and another began. We shared a quiet kiss. When we chose to spend the rest of the evening together, I closed the bedroom door and let the music fade into the background.\n\nThere were still things I needed to learn. There would be disagreements, bad days, repairs, and moments when one of us ran toward silence instead of conversation.\n\nBut there would also be pancakes. Chairs. Music. Open doors. A man who asked before touching me and listened when I answered.\n\nI had arrived in Beach City with one suitcase and no plan.\n\nNow I had a key in my pocket, Greg's hand in mine, and a home that did not ask me to earn the right to stay.\n\nEverything else could be written one song at a time.\n\nWeeks later, Greg played the song at the community center. This time, I stood beside him without waiting to be invited. The audience sang the chorus back, not perfectly, but loudly enough to make him laugh.\n\nAfterward, he handed me the microphone.\n\n“Your turn,” he said.\n\nI told the room about the first night in Beach City: the motel curtains, the lemon tea, the man with a guitar who carried my suitcase without taking my choices.\n\nGreg listened with his hand over his heart.\n\n“You are making me sound better than I am,” he whispered.\n\n“I know the worst tea you’ve ever made.”\n\n“A private matter.”\n\n“It belongs in the biography.”\n\nThe crowd laughed.\n\nWhen the lights went down, We walked home together. The van waited outside the apartment. My key opened the door. His key opened it too.\n\nInside, the record player began another song.\n\nGreg held out his hand.\n\n“Dance?”\n\n“You are terrible at it.”\n\n“I am committed.”\n\n“That is not the same thing.”\n\n“It is enough.”\n\nI took his hand.\n\nThe song carried me around the kitchen, past the stars on the wall, past the photograph and the couch and the life I had built from small choices.\n\nWhen the music ended, Greg kissed me.\n\nThe future remained unwritten.\n\nI was no longer afraid of that.\n\nThe song continued after the final chord. It lived in the community center, in the van, in the kitchen, and in every ordinary morning that followed.\n\nGreg still asked before touching me.\n\nI still made him ask twice when I wanted to tease him.\n\nThe ocean still forgot my footprints and made room for new ones.\n\nAnd when the future changed, I changed with it—together, by choice.\n\nGreg's hand found mine again.\n\n“Still here?” he asked.\n\n“Still here.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nThe ocean answered from beyond the buildings, patient as a heartbeat.\n\nThe day moved forward. The song ended, but the story did not.\n\nI had no perfect promise, only a person who kept asking and a heart that kept answering.\n\nGreg squeezed my hand.\n\n“Tomorrow?” he asked.\n\n“Tomorrow,” I said.\n\nThen We went home together.\n\nThe door opened with the key in my pocket. The record player waited. Greg set the pastries on the counter, and the ordinary evening began.\n\nWe ate together, then danced until the kitchen lights blurred. The future could wait until morning.\n\nMorning would bring another song.\n\nWe would sing it together.\n\nThe melody would be warm.\n\nAnd it would be mine.","word_count":1776},"su-33":{"number":33,"title":"A Door in the Wall","subtitle":"Stars Between the Strings · Chapter 33 of 40","content":"The door looked like part of the wall.\n\nI'd seen it every time I went into the garage, but it had been painted the same faded blue as the paneling. It didn't have a handle, just a small latch hidden behind a shelf of spare parts.\n\nGreg found it while looking for a replacement fuse. He stared at the latch for so long I thought he'd forgotten what we'd come to do.\n\n\"Oh,\" he said. \"That room.\"\n\n\"What room?\"\n\n\"The storage room.\"\n\n\"I thought the storage room was the one with the lawn chairs.\"\n\n\"That's the other storage room.\"\n\n\"How many are there?\"\n\n\"Apparently one more than I remember.\"\n\nHe opened the door. The hinges protested, and a smell of cardboard and damp paper drifted out. We found a stack of boxes, an old suitcase with one broken clasp, and posters rolled into tubes. A small lightbulb hung from a wire near the ceiling, but Greg refused to turn it on until someone had inspected the aged socket.\n\n\"Good call,\" I said, switching on my flashlight.\n\n\"See? Responsible adult.\"\n\nWe moved the first box into the clean part of the garage. Greg opened it carefully. Inside were old music flyers, photographs and a letter addressed in handwriting he recognized before he said anything.\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n\"My parents.\"\n\nI didn't reach for the letter. \"Do you want to read it?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"We don't have to decide today.\"\n\nHe laughed once, without humor. \"It's been in a box for years. You'd think I'd have made up my mind.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to change your mind more than once.\"\n\nHe sat on the folding stool. I sat on a crate beside him, leaving enough space for him to put the envelope down. The afternoon sun caught dust in the doorway. Somewhere outside, somebody was washing a car with a radio turned up too loud.\n\nHe opened the letter eventually. I watched his face, not the words.\n\n\"They wanted me to come home,\" he said. \"But only if I was willing to be someone else when I got there.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell me more?\"\n\nHe showed me one sentence, then folded the page again. That was all I needed to read.\n\n\"I left because I couldn't make them proud without disappearing,\" he said. \"And now I keep thinking maybe I should have been more patient.\"\n\n\"Maybe,\" I said. \"Or maybe you were patient for a long time and leaving was the only thing you knew how to do.\"\n\nHe leaned back against the wall.\n\n\"I don't want to hate them forever.\"\n\n\"Then don't promise yourself that you have to. You also don't have to forgive them today.\"\n\nThe smallest box had been pushed behind an old amplifier. Greg picked it up and almost dropped it when the bottom sagged.\n\n\"Okay. That's a terrible box.\"\n\nI fetched a clean one from the garage and helped him move the contents: two concert tickets, a bundle of receipts from places that no longer existed, and a postcard with no message on the back.\n\n\"Do you want to keep those?\" I asked.\n\n\"I don't know. Some of them remind me of good nights. Some remind me of trying too hard to look happy.\"\n\n\"Then we can label them undecided.\"\n\nHe looked at me as though I'd suggested a new form of magic.\n\n\"That allowed?\"\n\n\"It's your storage room.\"\n\nHe grinned briefly. Then he found the family letter beneath the receipts and went quiet again.\n\nI didn't reach for it. There was a difference between helping somebody sort a room and demanding access to every secret hidden in its drawers.\n\n\"Would you mind if I put the letter somewhere dry?\" he asked.\n\n\"Not at all. Do you want me to read it?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nHe set it in a clean sleeve and placed it near the top of the box. The rest of the room could stay messy for another afternoon. The wiring still needed a qualified electrician before we used the old outlet.\n\nGreg spent several minutes sorting the remaining papers into piles that seemed nonsensical until I asked what he was doing.\n\n\"Stuff I want to keep, stuff I need to think about, and stuff that probably shouldn't live next to an open window.\"\n\n\"That last category sounds surprisingly sensible.\"\n\n\"Hey, I run a car wash. Water damage is a professional concern.\"\n\nHe pulled an old photograph from the bottom of a box and turned it over before I could see it. Not to hide it from me, I thought, but because he wanted to decide how it would be handled.\n\n\"That's Steven when he was little,\" he said. \"I keep forgetting he doesn't fit in my arms like that anymore.\"\n\n\"Do you want to show me?\"\n\n\"Not yet. Maybe later.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nHe smiled at the simple answer, then looked at the letter again.\n\n\"My parents thought music was something I'd grow out of,\" he said. \"Steven grew up with a lot more freedom than I did. Sometimes I worry I overshot.\"\n\n\"You could talk to him about that.\"\n\n\"I do. He's got opinions.\" Greg grinned. \"A whole album of them.\"\n\nI sat beside him and helped close the boxes he had marked for storage. None of them required him to make an immediate decision about forgiveness. When he found his old guitar strap, he tested the buckle and put it in a pile for repair rather than treating it as a relic.\n\n\"You know what?\" he said. \"I think I'd rather go see my kid this weekend than stay here making myself miserable over old paper.\"\n\n\"That sounds like a plan.\"\n\nHe checked his phone before assuming I was coming with him. I liked that he asked me, and I liked that I could answer honestly. Family wasn't a subplot he performed for me. It belonged to him even when I wasn't in the room.\n\nWhen we reached the doorway, he looked back.\n\n\"I keep thinking you won't understand why I want any of this.\"\n\n\"I don't have to understand every object to respect the person who wants to keep it.\"\n\nHe smiled awkwardly. \"That's kind of romantic for a box of bad memories.\"\n\n\"I contain multitudes. Mostly labeled in terrible handwriting.\"\n\nHe laughed, then leaned close enough that his shoulder touched mine.\n\n\"I know you have people you loved before me, and people you love now,\" he said. \"You've been honest about ethical non-monogamy and preferring polyamory. I guess I was scared you needed me to prove I was done with my past too.\"\n\n\"You don't. I want you here as you are, not with all the older rooms erased.\"\n\nHe took my hand. We shut the storeroom light off, left the door unlocked for the electrician's scheduled visit, and went to make food. It was enough for today.\n\nWe sorted the boxes slowly. Greg kept one absurd poster of himself with a hairstyle so ambitious I had to sit down laughing. He kept a scratched guitar strap and a small photograph of a concert where he'd been happy. He put the family letter into a sealed folder instead of burning it or hanging it above the bed.\n\nI pointed to the broken suitcase.\n\n\"Is that trash?\"\n\n\"I thought so.\"\n\nHe lifted it, found an old train ticket caught in the lining, and changed his mind.\n\n\"Maybe it can hold the stuff I'm not ready to decide about.\"\n\n\"That sounds useful.\"\n\nHe looked at me. \"You okay with it being here?\"\n\n\"Why wouldn't I be?\"\n\n\"Because the room is full of a life you weren't in.\"\n\n\"Greg, I have a life beyond this room too. I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. Loving someone doesn't mean acquiring the right to erase everybody who came before or everybody else who's here now.\"\n\nHe considered that, then smiled a little. \"I keep forgetting how sensible you are.\"\n\n\"That is a wildly inaccurate description.\"\n\n\"Still.\" He took my hand. \"Thanks for not making me choose between throwing this away and pretending it never hurt.\"\n\nWe carried a few salvageable boxes out to dry and left the wet ones on a plastic sheet for inspection. We didn't store anything against the old electrical outlet. Greg made a note to pay for an electrician before using the room, which was a distinctly unglamorous way to end a discovery.\n\nOne of the posters had a name scrawled across the back. Greg saw it and sat down on an upturned crate.\n\n“That's Marty’s handwriting,” he said.\n\n“The manager?”\n\n“Yeah. Old manager. Old mistakes. Probably a little of both.”\n\nHe rubbed his thumb across the edge of the cardboard, careful not to smear the ink. For a moment I thought he was going to tell me the entire history. Instead he folded the poster over and tucked it beneath the others.\n\n“You can keep it without explaining it to me,” I said.\n\n“That's kind of the thing,” he answered. “I want to explain some of it. I just don't want it to become a whole trial where I have to prove I was either a great guy or a complete idiot.”\n\nI shifted closer. The room smelled of wet paper, old amplifier dust and motor oil. There was nowhere dignified to sit, so I lowered myself onto the clean part of the floor.\n\n“Maybe you were both,” I said.\n\nHe made a face. “Gee, thanks.”\n\n“Not at the same time. Possibly.”\n\n“Better.”\n\nHe told me about a night when he'd been sure a bad contract was the only way to get a band on the road, how he'd signed before reading everything because he was afraid of being told no. He didn't blame Rose for the choices he made before he met her, and he didn't turn his old manager into an excuse for everything that followed.\n\n“I wish somebody'd told me I could walk away,” he said.\n\n“Would you have listened?”\n\n“Absolutely not.” He grinned, but it faded. “Maybe later.”\n\nWe agreed to photograph the poster before deciding whether to keep it. The photo would be private. Greg didn't owe the wall a shrine, and he didn't owe me the inside of every memory.\n\nAs I stood, my hand slipped on the crate. He caught my elbow, then immediately let go when I had my balance.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\n“It's okay. The floor's not my finest dance partner.”\n\n“It's got two left feet,” he said. “And somehow, both of them are mine.”\n\nI laughed, and he laughed too. We left the old papers where they could be sorted on a day when neither of us was tired of being brave.\n\nAs evening came, the shadows stretched across the garage. He stood in the doorway with the letter folder in his hand.\n\n\"Do you want some company tonight?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah. I do.\"\n\nWe went into the house and made sandwiches. He told me a story about the first poster he'd hung as a teenager, and how he'd been certain it would make him famous. I told him I once kept a ridiculous souvenir because I couldn't bear to admit the trip had been a disappointment. We laughed about being younger and less certain.\n\nHe leaned close but stopped.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nIt wasn't a cure for the letter. It wasn't supposed to be. His mouth was warm, his hand a little shaky against my cheek. I moved closer, and he let out a breath he seemed to have been holding for years.\n\nLater, we closed the curtains and let the evening be ours. The storage-room door remained open, its light switched off, with the boxes labeled for another day.\n\nThe next morning Greg added a second note to the electrician's list: *Ask before moving stored papers.* He showed it to me without making a speech.\n\nI liked that more than a promise to forget.","word_count":1980},"su-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Night After the Show","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fourth expansion pass","content":"The last cable had tangled itself around Greg's ankle.\n\nThat was the only explanation I could find for why a man who had spent decades handling concert equipment was standing in the middle of Starwake Pier looking personally betrayed by a length of black cord.\n\n\"I swear it wasn't like that when I put it down,\" he said.\n\nI looked at the knot.\n\n\"Maybe the cable has a private life.\"\n\n\"Very funny.\"\n\n\"It could be ethically non-monogamous. Perhaps it's seeing the speaker cable and the microphone stand.\"\n\nHe laughed so hard he had to lean against the equipment case.\n\n\"Don't tell me you're jealous of a cable.\"\n\n\"Only of how much attention you're giving it.\"\n\nHe grinned and reached for my hand, but the cable tightened around his boot.\n\n\"Hold that thought.\"\n\nI bent down and unwound the loop.\n\nThe Listening Fair had ended an hour earlier. The crowd was gone, the tea table had been cleaned, and the donation box had been counted with Mara present. The temporary lights were off except for the safety lanterns along the accessible path.\n\nWe had agreed to finish the cleanup before leaving.\n\nGreg had agreed very enthusiastically.\n\nNow he was trying to make an enemy out of electrical equipment.\n\n\"Maybe you should write a song about it,\" I said.\n\n\"I already have three verses.\"\n\n\"Of course you do.\"\n\nHe hummed something ridiculous as we packed the cables. I joined in with a deliberately awful harmony. His smile grew until he couldn't keep the melody straight.\n\nIt was the kind of evening I liked best with him. No performance required, no audience expecting us to turn love into a single perfect story. Just the two of us, a lot of equipment, and the small satisfaction of work actually finished.\n\nWe carried the last case to the storage shed.\n\nI checked the lock.\n\nGreg checked it again.\n\n\"You're learning from Danse,\" I said.\n\n\"Hey, his system works.\"\n\n\"Only after we removed the part that needed a ladder.\"\n\nGreg laughed.\n\nWe started toward the Starwake Door.\n\nThe night air was cool enough that I pulled my coat tighter. He noticed without making a fuss and offered me the spare scarf he'd brought. It was faded blue, soft from years of use, and smelled faintly of laundry soap.\n\n\"Thanks,\" I said.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nHe slipped an arm around my shoulders as we walked.\n\nFor a while we didn't talk. The sound of the sea moved beneath the pier. Lanterns glowed along the path back to Lanternwell Square, where a few late visitors were making their way home from the other districts.\n\nThen Greg stopped.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Are you happy?\"\n\nThe question was so simple that I didn't know how to answer it.\n\n\"Right now?\"\n\n\"Yeah. Right now.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"I'm tired. My feet hurt. I have a notebook full of repairs we still need to schedule. And I'm happy.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Are you?\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nHe looked toward the dark water.\n\n\"I used to think happy meant getting to the part after everything difficult. The concert's over, the money's enough, everybody's okay, nobody's upset.\"\n\n\"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It was.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb over my knuckles.\n\n\"Now I think it can just be this. A night where we did something good and still have a few problems to deal with tomorrow.\"\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n\"That's a very nice way to say we have chores.\"\n\n\"Romantic, right?\"\n\n\"Unbelievably.\"\n\nHe kissed my cheek.\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Was that all you had planned?\"\n\nHe raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"I could do better.\"\n\n\"Could you?\"\n\n\"Challenge accepted.\"\n\nHe kissed me properly this time, warm and slow, the kind that made the cold air feel less important. His hand rested lightly at my waist. I held the edge of his jacket and smiled against his mouth.\n\nWhen we parted, he looked a little breathless.\n\n\"Better?\"\n\n\"Much.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nNola caught us before we left. Her sleeves were pushed to her elbows, and she'd been carrying the same clipboard since the first soundcheck.\n\n\"Greg, you're done,\" she said.\n\n\"I am aware.\"\n\n\"Then stop taking cables out of other people's hands.\"\n\nHe blinked at the coil he'd just reached for. \"I thought I was helping.\"\n\nNola pointed to the work roster taped above the storage shelf.\n\n\"See that name?\" she asked.\n\nGreg leaned closer. \"Tamsin.\"\n\n\"She's responsible for that section. If you take the cable out of her hands, she has to check your work before she can clock out.\"\n\nHe dropped both hands to his sides. \"Wow. I thought I was being useful.\"\n\n\"You were useful during your soundcheck. Now you can be useful by not creating another soundcheck.\"\n\nI bit my lip. Greg caught me trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Don't,\" he warned.\n\n\"I said nothing.\"\n\n\"Your face said a whole verse.\"\n\nHe took out his phone, sent a quick thanks to the stage crew and offered to pay for a replacement cable he'd damaged. Nola checked the inventory before accepting. The cable was still sound, and nobody needed a public performance of guilt.\n\nOutside, he put his arm around my shoulders only after I leaned into him.\n\n\"Think I've got a songwriting problem?\" he asked.\n\n\"Only when your lyrics start turning people into chores.\"\n\n\"New title: The Ballad of Letting Other Adults Do Their Jobs.\"\n\n\"Needs a better chorus.\"\n\n\"Yeah, probably.\"\n\nWe walked toward the sheltered path. He stopped beneath a lantern where the light made his hair look almost copper.\n\n\"Can I kiss you before I come up with another terrible title?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nThe kiss was warm and a little ridiculous; he smiled against my mouth before we were finished. We still had to check the set list tomorrow, and somebody else still owned the cleanup shift. For a moment I could enjoy him without trying to make our affection responsible for the whole pier.\n\n\"You are. But Lio's crew is paid for cleanup and you have an actual life to go home to.\"\n\nGreg looked at me, his hair falling into his eyes. \"Do I?\"\n\n\"Allegedly.\"\n\nNola checked a box on the sheet. \"Also, your guitar case is in the wrong storage bay.\"\n\n\"You're destroying my reputation.\"\n\n\"The case weighs more than the small instrument shelf can safely hold.\"\n\nHe took it without arguing. I loved that his first response was a joke and his second was to fix the problem. Not every lesson needed a solemn speech.\n\nOutside, we stood under the covered path while a drizzle began to spot the lantern glass. Greg watched the crew finish rolling the last cable without him. He looked oddly lonely for a moment.\n\n\"I always used to stay until the last person left,\" he said. \"Then I could tell myself I'd been necessary.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Mostly it meant I was too tired to enjoy the ride home.\"\n\nHe tucked his guitar safely under the awning. \"You know I'm still gonna worry when you travel to see the others.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But you've told me you're ethically non-monogamous and you prefer polyamory. I don't want to make you prove you care by missing your own plans.\"\n\nI caught the front of his jacket, tugging him close enough for our foreheads to touch.\n\n\"You can tell me you miss me,\" I said. \"I don't mind. I just don't want missing me to become a verdict.\"\n\nHe smiled. \"That's the least catchy song title I ever heard.\"\n\n\"It'll be a hit.\"\n\nHe hummed a deliberately dreadful chorus under his breath. I laughed, and he kissed the corner of my mouth before asking whether I wanted to go back to Beach City. I did. Nola's clipboard remained at Starwake, in the hands of somebody who knew exactly where the cables belonged.\n\nWe went through the Starwake Door into Beach City, where the van waited beneath a streetlamp. The familiar smell of the coast met me: salt, asphalt, and somebody's late-night food from a nearby shop.\n\nGreg opened the van and set down his guitar.\n\n\"Want to stay?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked at me for another moment.\n\n\"Because you want to?\"\n\n\"Because I want to.\"\n\nHe smiled, relief and affection moving together across his face.\n\nInside, we folded the blanket, put the equipment list on the dashboard, and made a small space where there had been too many things. Greg found a packet of crackers and offered them with great ceremony.\n\n\"A feast.\"\n\n\"Truly, you spoil me.\"\n\n\"I try.\"\n\nWe ate the crackers and laughed about the fair. He imitated Toph declaring the practice platform \"almost competent.\" I tried to imitate Iroh's calm reply and failed because I started laughing halfway through the sentence.\n\nThen the laughter settled.\n\nGreg reached for my hand.\n\n\"I know you have other people you love,\" he said quietly. \"I know we talked about ethical non-monogamy and polyamory, and I know it doesn't make tonight less ours.\"\n\nI squeezed his fingers.\n\n\"It doesn't.\"\n\n\"I just wanted to say that I like having nights that belong to us because we choose them.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe leaned close.\n\nThe next kiss was unhurried. There was no need to fill the silence with a joke, though I knew he would if I asked. I rested my forehead against his and listened to the small sounds of the van cooling in the night.\n\n\"Stay with me,\" he whispered.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nWe turned down the light and let the rest of the evening be private.\n\nIn the morning, sunlight came through the windshield and woke me before the alarm. Greg was still asleep, one hand resting near the folded equipment list. His hair looked as though it had been involved in a disagreement with a pillow.\n\nI smiled.\n\nI was tempted to let him sleep.\n\nThen I remembered that the repair team was expecting the donation total by nine.\n\nI nudged his shoulder.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nA muffled sound.\n\n\"Greg, we have responsibilities.\"\n\n\"Tell them I'm a guitar.\"\n\n\"That makes no sense.\"\n\n\"Great guitars don't do paperwork.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe opened one eye.\n\n\"Coffee?\"\n\n\"After the totals are sent.\"\n\n\"Brutal.\"\n\nWe made coffee anyway, then sat together at the small table outside the van and checked the ledger. The donations covered the new fasteners and some of the lumber, exactly as we'd announced. We sent the figures to Tamsin and copied the volunteers who had helped. No exaggeration. No promise that the rest of the repairs would happen by magic.\n\nGreg looked at the final number.\n\n\"Not everything we wanted.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Enough for the next step.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe leaned over and kissed my temple.\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\nGreg checked the payment ledger a second time before we left the van. One of the lines for the sound technician was marked *volunteer honorarium*, which sounded suspiciously like money somebody might decide not to pay.\n\n“Hold up,” he said. “The technician was hired to check the power. That's not an honorarium.”\n\nThe person at the desk said the fundraiser had been short on cash and that perhaps she would accept a credit on the program.\n\nGreg stared at the paper. “I love a good credit. Can't exactly put it in the gas tank, though.”\n\nHe asked for the original work order. It listed a fixed inspection fee, agreed before the event. The technician had already done the work and passed the stage on the condition that the unsafe circuit remain closed.\n\n“We owe the fee,” Greg said. “It's not a favor we can turn into applause.”\n\nThere was some uncomfortable arithmetic. Nola checked which donations had been restricted to the rail repairs, then called the committee member who had approved the technician's booking. They agreed to cover the invoice from the event operating account and carry the lower railing balance forward honestly.\n\nGreg looked tired when it was done, the kind of tired no encore would cure.\n\n“You okay?” I asked.\n\n“Yeah. Just realizing a musician isn't a charity because somebody printed 'benefit' on the poster.”\n\n“You've done a lot.”\n\n“So have you. And you're allowed to go home before the chairs.”\n\nI pressed my forehead against his shoulder. He hummed the first four notes of our unfinished song, badly out of tune, and I laughed into his jacket.\n\nWe still had people to thank and a real repair to finish. We didn't need to make anybody's unpaid labor the price of the good night we'd had.\n\nBy the time we returned to Starwake Pier, the volunteers were already moving supplies to the safe storage area. Toph had left a note about checking the platform after the next rain. Iroh had sent a small tin of the new house tea blend as thanks.\n\nWe got to work.\n\nThat was the best part of the night after the show: the morning wasn't a punishment for being happy.\n\nIt was simply the next part of our life.","word_count":2144}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="stars-between-the-strings");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=82453;})();
